window.INO_POSTS=[{"slug":"the-joy-of-learning-is-getting-lost","t":"The Joy of Learning Is Getting Lost","url":"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/the-joy-of-learning-is-getting-lost/","author":"Arjun Krishna","d":"2026-09-13","tags":["Free Thinking"],"img":"https://pub-5911fd2c1cb64c8bb9ae3e5ffbb142b8.r2.dev/2026/09/PR57KGT_S17J34Qkrupg.jpg","mins":10,"html":"<p>I still remember my first website. It wasn't anything impressive. It was a simple landing page, something like a coming-soon page that I was building before eventually creating a portfolio website. I remember referring to YouTube videos and tutorials, trying to figure out HTML and CSS, and spending ridiculous amounts of time trying to get simple things to work.</p><p>I even remember the image I used. It was a wallpaper I had downloaded from <a href=\"http://pexels.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Pexels</a>, with two horses running in the background. I remember the font too. <a href=\"https://www.dafontfree.io/gilroy-font-family/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Gilroy</a>.</p><blockquote>It's strange what your brain chooses to remember.</blockquote><p>Years later, I don't remember the code I wrote for that website. I don't remember which CSS properties I struggled with or how many times I broke the layout. But I remember those two horses. I remember the font. I remember what the page looked like. Most importantly, I remember how it felt to finally see something I had imagined actually appear in the browser.</p><p>That was the joy. And lately, I've been wondering where that feeling went.</p><h2 id=\"i-build-things-for-the-fun-of-it\">I Build Things for the Fun of It</h2><p>I've been writing code for a long time now. I write code for a living. I'm a software engineer. But I've never really considered programming to be just my job.</p><p>I genuinely like writing code and building things. When I'm done with work, I often go back to my editor and start building something for myself. Sometimes it's something I want to learn, sometimes an experiment, and sometimes just an idea that makes me wonder, \"Can I actually build this?\"</p><p>For me, building things has always been a hobby. Some people watch movies or play games. I open my editor and start building something that probably doesn't need to exist.</p><p>If you look through my GitHub, you'll find a lot of small-small projects. Most aren't complete. <a href=\"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/author/nikhil/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Nikhil</a>, <a href=\"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/author/abhishek/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Abhishek</a>, <a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/nithindaniel/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Nithin</a> and all those who knew me from Inovus Labs, have all asked me some version of the same question at some point: \"Why don't you just finish one of these and turn it into something useful?\"</p><blockquote>But that's usually not why I started them. Most projects exist because I wanted to learn, try, understand or experiment with something. <strong>The project is just a reason to do that. Once I've figured out the thing I actually cared about, I'm usually done.</strong></blockquote><p>That's why so many of them are unfinished. But I still remember them. I remember what I was trying to learn, the stupid bugs, the things that didn't work, and the moment when they finally did.</p><h2 id=\"learning-used-to-take-time\">Learning used to take time</h2><p>I still remember learning <a href=\"https://nodejs.org/en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Node.js</a>. It took me a couple of weeks of watching tutorials, reading documentation, searching for answers, trying things, breaking things and fixing them. Eventually, I started building something with it.</p><p><u>I kept learning because there was always something else I wanted to build, and there was something beautiful about that process</u>. You didn't know something, so you tried to understand it. You got stuck, searched for an answer, tried again, broke something else, fixed it, and eventually something clicked.</p><p>That moment was the reward. The final application wasn't the only thing that mattered. The journey mattered too. The struggle was part of the experience.</p><h2 id=\"then-ai-happened\">Then AI happened</h2><p>AI didn't arrive all at once.</p><p>My first code editor was <a href=\"https://atom-editor.cc/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Atom</a>. Then I moved to <a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">VS Code</a>, and I still remember seeing its syntax completion and thinking how amazing it was.</p><p>Then came <a href=\"https://github.com/features/copilot\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GitHub Copilot</a>. I had the student subscription and started using it. Suddenly, it wasn't just completing syntax anymore. It could understand what I was trying to build and suggest entire pieces of code. That felt like a whole new level.</p><p>Then came <a href=\"https://chatgpt.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ChatGPT</a>. You could actually talk to a machine about what you were trying to build and ask questions that would previously have meant searching </p><p>Then came more capable models, coding assistants like <a href=\"https://cursor.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Cursor</a> and <a href=\"https://antigravity.google/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Antigravity</a>, and eventually coding agents like <a href=\"https://claude.com/product/claude-code\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Claude Code</a> and <a href=\"https://opencode.ai/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenCode</a>.</p><p>At first, AI <u>helped me write code</u>. Then it <u>helped me understand code</u>. Then it <u>started writing larger pieces</u> of the application, <u>entire features</u>, and <u>eventually entire projects</u>. Today, you can describe what you want and watch an AI build it. It can write the code, create and run tests, find problems, fix them, commit changes and create pull requests.</p><blockquote>The distance between \"I have an idea\" and \"I have something working\" has become ridiculously small.</blockquote><p>That is objectively incredible. But somewhere along the way, I started missing something.</p><h2 id=\"i-miss-making-mistakes\">I miss making mistakes</h2><p>I miss making mistakes. <u>Not because mistakes are good, but because of what came after them</u>. There was something satisfying about spending hours figuring out why something wasn't working, digging through documentation and forums, and eventually finding the answer. Because you struggled to understand it, you remembered it.</p><p>One of the clearest examples for me was <a href=\"https://go.dev/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Go</a>. I had already learned it over a weekend, but when I came back to it with AI, I could get through the fundamentals and start building something in roughly thirty minutes. I could ask questions, get explanations, check examples, fix problems, and keep moving.</p><p>That's amazing, but there was a strange emptiness to it. There was no particular bug I would remember years later, no concept that took an entire evening to understand, no moment where something finally clicked after being stuck for hours.</p><blockquote>There is a difference between knowing something and experiencing the process of learning it.</blockquote><p>I can understand a concept without spending three days struggling with it. I can know how something works without building it from scratch. I can produce something without experiencing the journey that used to lead to that result. Maybe that's perfectly fine. But I miss the journey.</p><h2 id=\"code-used-to-have-fingerprints\">Code used to have fingerprints</h2><p>There is another thing I've started noticing.</p><p>Human beings have quirks. Developers are human too — despite what some people think. 😄 I remember working with people where I could look at a piece of code and have a pretty good idea who among the team wrote it, without checking Git history. Someone preferred <code>async/await</code>, someone else preferred Promise chains, someone had a particular way of structuring functions, and someone else had strange variable names.</p><p>Some of those habits were objectively bad, but they were human. They were fingerprints.</p><blockquote>AI-generated code tends to smooth a lot of that away. It is often cleaner, more consistent and more predictable, and that's good. AI can reduce bugs, make code more maintainable, remove boilerplate and help teams ship faster.</blockquote><p>But something gets lost when everyone's code starts looking the same. <strong>The code becomes cleaner and more consistent, but also a little less personal.</strong></p><h2 id=\"i-am-part-of-the-problem\">I am part of the problem</h2><p>The funny thing is, I'm not sitting here pretending I don't use AI.</p><p>I use it a lot. I use it at work, I use it for my own projects, and I genuinely enjoy having these tools around. I've watched them evolve from Copilot and ChatGPT to coding assistants and increasingly capable <strong>agent harnesses</strong>, and I've happily tried almost every step along the way.</p><p>I've written about this before, from different angles. This time, I'm looking at it from a much more personal perspective. Have a look at the two posts below.</p><figure><a href=\"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/faster-ai-worse-engineering/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">We Reached the Moon with Kilobytes. What Went Wrong After That?<small>We carry more computing power in our pockets than NASA did during Apollo. Yet our apps lag, leak memory, and waste resources. As AI accelerates development, are we becoming better engineers — or just scaling inefficiency?</small></a></figure><figure><a href=\"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/ai-tools-impact-on-next-gen-devs/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Is AI Assistance Killing the Next Generation of Developers?<small>AI tools are transforming coding, but over-reliance risks making us spectators, not creators. While AI can optimize and assist, the spark of problem-solving and logic must stay ours. Let AI guide, not replace, our creativity—because true development lies in understanding, not just prompting.</small></a></figure><p>That's what makes this whole thing a little uncomfortable.</p><blockquote>I don't want AI to go away. I don't even want to go back to the way things were. <strong>I just sometimes miss the way I used to learn and build things before AI</strong> became this good.</blockquote><p>I'm not saying this because I hate AI. I love building things, and I'm just starting to wonder whether, in making everything easier, AI is taking away a part of the experience that made building things so enjoyable in the first place.</p><blockquote>So yes, you can call me a hypocrite.</blockquote><p>I'm using the very thing I'm worried about. And that's probably why I notice what it's taking away.</p><hr><h2 id=\"the-ai-race-is-getting-strange\">The AI race is getting strange</h2><p>And lately, the conversation around AI has started feeling a bit different.</p><blockquote>Just last week, it felt like the entire industry was racing at once. Anthropic released <a href=\"https://www.anthropic.com/claude-fable-and-mythos-5-1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Claude Fable 5.1 and Mythos 5.1</a>. The next day, Meta released <a href=\"https://research.meta.ai/blog/introducing-muse-spark-1-3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Muse Spark 1.3</a>. Then OpenAI came out with <a href=\"https://openai.com/index/gpt-6-astra-next-generation-work/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GPT-6 Astra</a>. Google also shipped <a href=\"https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/models-and-research/gemini-models/3-8-flash-and-3-8-flash-cyber/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">new Gemini models</a> in the middle of all of this.</blockquote><p>It wasn't just another week of new model releases. Each new model seemed to come with another jump in what these systems could actually do, especially around coding, reasoning and increasingly autonomous work.</p><p>And then the conversation around the pace of all this started getting even more interesting.</p><blockquote>On Friday, <strong>Jacob Coxon</strong>, an AI researcher who recently left Anthropic, <a href=\"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/technology/tech-news/former-anthropic-researcher-jacob-coxon-resigns-warns-ai-could-kill-us-all-by-the-end-of-the-decade-they-are-racing-straight-to-self-improving-superintelligence/articleshow/134029385.cms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">publicly warned that we may be entering a critical period for humanity as AI systems become more capable</a> and potentially start improving themselves.</blockquote><blockquote>Yesterday, Anthropic CEO <strong>Dario Amodei</strong> called for the <a href=\"https://darioamodei.com/post/we-must-pace-the-frontier\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">industry to slow down frontier AI development</a> and put stronger safety measures in place.</blockquote><blockquote>What caught my attention was what happened next. <a href=\"https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/sam-altman-elon-musk-back-anthropic-ceo-dario-amodei-s-warning-to-slow-ai-development-101789237991169.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><strong>Sam Altman agreed with Amodei</strong>, saying OpenAI needed to \"pace the frontier.\" <strong>Elon Musk backed him too, simply saying, \"Dario is right.\"</strong></a> It was a rare moment where leaders of rival AI companies agreed that the pace itself was worth worrying about.</blockquote><p>I'm not saying AI is going to destroy humanity. I don't know what happens next, and neither does anyone else with certainty. <strong>But when the people building these systems themselves start publicly asking whether we're moving too fast, I think it's worth paying attention.</strong></p><p>Because while I'm sitting here worrying about whether AI is taking away the joy of learning, there are people asking a much bigger question: <strong>what happens when these systems become capable of doing things we no longer fully understand or control?</strong></p><p>And somewhere between those two questions, I keep wondering what exactly we're optimizing for.</p><h2 id=\"what-are-we-optimizing-for\">What are we optimizing for?</h2><p>There is a scene in <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WALL-E\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><em>WALL-E</em></a> that I keep thinking about. Humans have become so dependent on convenience that they barely need to do anything themselves.</p><figure></figure><p>I'm not saying AI will turn us into the humans from <em>WALL-E</em>. But the idea does make me wonder what happens when we stop doing things simply because we don't have to anymore.</p><p>I don't want to go back to spending three hours searching for a missing semicolon or reading fifty Stack Overflow answers to understand something an AI can explain in thirty seconds. I love what AI has made possible. The barrier between having an idea and building something has never been lower.</p><blockquote>But humans aren't machines optimized purely for efficiency. We also want experiences, stories and mastery. We want to become good at things and remember what it felt like when something was difficult.<strong> Thanks to </strong><a href=\"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/author/nikhil/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><strong>Nikhil</strong></a><strong>, who keeps reminding me of that.</strong></blockquote><p>Maybe the answer isn't to stop using AI. Maybe it's just to be a little more intentional about what we choose to learn and build. There are things where AI can do a lot for us, and there are things where it can't do nearly as much.</p><blockquote>Lately, I've been getting interested in things like <a href=\"https://hamradioprep.com/what-is-ham-radio/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ham radio</a>, where there is still a lot to learn by actually doing it. I've also been watching <a href=\"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/author/arjun-a-acharry/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Arjun A Acharry</a> get into <a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/posts/arjun-a-acharry_dronetechnology-drones-education-activity-7490781000558403584-PLYH\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">drone technology</a>. There's something <strong>really appreciable about seeing him explore something completely outside his usual domain, and it encourages me to do the same</strong>.</blockquote><p>Maybe that's the kind of thing I need more of. Things where the experience itself matters, where you have to learn, experiment, make mistakes and figure things out yourself. Sometimes, efficiency just isn't the point.</p><h2 id=\"i-miss-the-horses\">I miss the horses</h2><p>That first website probably looks terrible by today's standards. If I opened it now, I'd probably want to rewrite everything with better typography, better CSS and a modern framework. I'd probably ask an AI to improve it.</p><p>But I don't want to. That website isn't special because it was good. It's special because I made it. I remember the horses, the Gilroy font, searching for tutorials, not knowing what I was doing, and finally seeing the page in the browser.</p><p>I don't remember the code anymore. I remember the process.</p><p>AI has made building easier than ever, and I don't want to give that up. I just want to keep some of that process too.</p><p>There was something satisfying about finally looking at the screen and thinking:</p><p><strong>I made this.</strong></p><p>I miss that feeling. And I hope I'm not the only one.</p>"},{"slug":"i-locked-myself-out-of-postgresql-trying-to-make-it-secure-building-a-secure-postgresql-server-with-docker-cloudflare-r2-part-1","t":"I Locked Myself Out of PostgreSQL Trying to Make It Secure Building a Secure PostgreSQL Server with Docker & Cloudflare R2 — Part 1","url":"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/i-locked-myself-out-of-postgresql-trying-to-make-it-secure-building-a-secure-postgresql-server-with-docker-cloudflare-r2-part-1/","author":"Amith Abey Stephen","d":"2026-09-06","tags":["Project @ Inovus","Science & Technology"],"img":"https://pub-5911fd2c1cb64c8bb9ae3e5ffbb142b8.r2.dev/2026/09/ChatGPT-Image-Sep-5--2026--06_20_57-PM.png","mins":5,"html":"<p>Well.... this ain't a big story or something new. But honestly, I feel this is one of those things that clears out the basics we should actually learn.</p><p>And this whole thing started because of our dear <a href=\"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/author/naveen/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Naveen Joseph</a><strong> Chettan</strong>.</p><p>If you've been around me for a while, you probably know this already,that weekly 3 dasy we get to meet each other in train while travelling to office.(This is the time when we just our deep talks- talks about the days, work, his home server setup) &amp; every time he talks about the setups he has in his home servers....The main thing i hear is security reasons: how he isolates things.... how he doesn't just expose everything to the internet.... how every service has its own access.... backups.... certificates.... all those things, but nothing leaves his network. </p><p>And every time he talks about it I'm like....</p><p>\"Ufff.... I should probably learn this, focus more on security side too rather than just simple copy paste.\" 😂 </p><p>For a long time i had this urge to do it in proper best way.So....</p><p>I finally decided to actually do it.</p><hr><h2 id=\"so-first-lets-get-the-basic-picture-clear\">So first.... let's get the basic picture clear.</h2><p>I have an Oracle Cloud server. That's my actual server.</p><p>And instead of installing PostgreSQL directly onto Ubuntu, I wanted PostgreSQL to run inside Docker.</p>💡For those who don’t know what PostgreSQL (Postgres) is: it’s a powerful SQL database system used to store, organize, and manage data for your projects. <a href=\"https://www.postgresql.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">More info.</a>💡And for docker: it’s a tool that packages your application and everything it needs to run into a <strong>container </strong>(consider it as a box or packet), making it easy to run the same application consistently on different computers and servers. <a href=\"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/docker-simplified/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><strong>More info.</strong></a><p>So the basic architecture was something like:</p><p><strong>Oracle Cloud Server</strong></p><p>↓ </p><p><strong>Docker</strong></p><p>↓</p><p><strong>PostgreSQL container</strong></p><p>↓</p><p><strong>Multiple databases</strong></p><p>And the goal wasn't just:</p><blockquote>\"Run PostgreSQL somewhere.\"</blockquote><p>I wanted to understand how to run it in a reasonably secure way. So I made myself a small checklist. I wanted:</p><ul><li>PostgreSQL running inside Docker</li><li>Don't expose PostgreSQL using the default port</li><li>Don't allow random direct access</li><li>Use SSL/TLS for secure connections (encrypts the data sent between your browser and a website)</li><li>Give every application its own database user</li><li>Make sure one application's user cannot access another application's database</li></ul><p>Sounds pretty clear now.</p><p>But....</p><p><strong>It wasn't clear to me at the beginning.</strong></p><p>And that became the biggest problem.</p><hr>Round 1 — I just followed ChatGPT<p>So I did what probably most people would do. I took all these requirements....gave them to ChatGPT.... and started following the instructions. Step by step. </p><p>Copy this. Create this file. Paste that. <br>Change this configuration. Run this command. <br>Restart Docker. Check PostgreSQL.</p><p>Change <code>pg_hba.conf</code>. Configure SSL. Create users. Create databases. Change ports.</p><p>Add permissions. Run another command.</p><p>And I kept going. Everything looked fine.... until it wasn't. 🥲</p><p>The problem wasn't that ChatGPT wasn't giving me commands.</p><p>The problem was <strong>I was executing commands without having a proper picture of what I was building.</strong></p><p>I knew individual pieces. But I didn't properly understand how all those pieces were supposed to fit together.</p><p>Individualy i had my exposed port a random one, configured ssl config, created users, db, etc gave permissons to user etc.....and it went on like too far that <strong>I couldn't properly access my own database anymore.</strong> 😭</p><p>Like i ran more commands without a clear end goal and when finaly it said its ready i tried creating a new db, but nah authentication failed. i was like bruh!!</p><hr><h2 id=\"i-knew-i-was-doing-it-wrong\">I knew I was doing it wrong</h2><p>I remember thinking:</p><blockquote>\"Okay.... I'm definitely doing something wrong here.\"</blockquote><p>But I kept trying to fix it, but my mindset was:</p><p><em>\"Well what am i doing, what do i need...what actually is my end goal!\"</em></p><p>Except I wasn't really figuring it out. I was patching things. And eventually....</p><p>I got frustrated. Closed everything.</p><p>At that point I understood almost nothing about what state the server was actually in. So instead of continuing to pile more configuration on top of a setup I didn't understand....</p><p>I decided to stop.</p><hr>Delete everything. Start again.<p>Later that day, I logged back into the Oracle server.</p><p>And this time I did something drastic. I cleared the Docker setup.</p><p>Removed the containers. Removed the PostgreSQL volume/data. Removed the configurations I had been experimenting with.</p><p>Basically....</p><p><strong>Fresh start.</strong></p><p>The server was still there. But the PostgreSQL/Docker setup was clean again. And honestly....</p><p>That felt much better.</p><p>Because now I wasn't trying to repair something I didn't understand. I could build it again. </p><p>But this time....I wanted to know what I was actually building <strong>before</strong> I started throwing commands at the terminal. Like a clear definition of my end goals.</p><hr>So I called Naveen chettan.<p>This was probably the most useful step of the whole experiment.</p><p>Instead of asking:</p><blockquote>\"Chetta, why is this command failing? Why am i not able to log back in even with correct credentials...\"</blockquote><p>I asked something much more important.</p><blockquote>\"What exactly should I build?\"</blockquote><p>I wanted to know:</p><ul><li>What's actually important here?</li><li>What should I secure?</li><li>What doesn't need to be over-engineered?</li><li>What should the final setup look like?</li><li>What should I understand myself?</li><li>And where am I just blindly following ChatGPT?</li></ul><p>That conversation changed the way I approached the whole thing.</p><p>Because I finally had an <strong>end goal</strong>.</p><p>Not:</p><blockquote>\"Make PostgreSQL secure.\"</blockquote><p>That's way too vague.</p><p>But something much more concrete.</p><hr>This is what I actually wanted<p>I wanted one Oracle server. Inside that server, Docker would run PostgreSQL. Something like:</p><pre><code>Oracle Cloud VM\n│\n└── Docker\n     │\n     └── PostgreSQL\n          │\n          ├── Database: syncbatch\n          │     └── User: syncbatch_user\n          │\n          ├── Database: another_app\n          │     └── User: another_app_user\n          │\n          └── Database: ...</code></pre><p>The PostgreSQL superuser would be the administrator. Application users would <strong>not</strong> be superusers. Each application would get its own database and its own credentials.</p><p>Then access to PostgreSQL from outside would be restricted and encrypted.</p><p>And finally....</p><p>PostgreSQL backups would be created on the server, temporarily stored locally, and then pushed to Cloudflare R2.</p><p>Something like:</p><pre><code>PostgreSQL\n    ↓\n pg_dump\n    ↓\nCompressed backup\n    ↓\nLocal backup folder\n    ↓\n   rclone\n    ↓\nCloudflare R2</code></pre><p>And later, cron could automate when that backup script runs.</p><p>Now....</p><p><strong>This made sense.</strong></p><p>I had clear idea of what each steps result is, Goal fo each step its final outcome. And that was the difference between my first attempt and the second one.</p><hr>So now.... let's actually build it.<p>With the end goal clear, I could finally start from the bottom.</p><p>First:</p><p><strong>The Oracle server.</strong></p><p>Then:</p><p><strong>Docker.</strong></p><p>Then:</p><p><strong>PostgreSQL.</strong></p><p>Then:</p><p><strong>Persistent storage.</strong></p><p>Then:</p><p><strong>PostgreSQL configuration.</strong></p><p>Then:</p><p><strong>SSL/TLS.</strong></p><p>Then:</p><p><strong>Admin authentication.</strong></p><p>Then:</p><p><strong>Database-specific users.</strong></p><p>Then:</p><p><strong>Permissions.</strong></p><p>Then:</p><p><strong>Backups.</strong></p><p>Then:</p><p><strong>Cloudflare R2.</strong></p><p>Then:</p><p><strong>rclone.</strong></p><p>And finally:</p><p><strong>Automation.</strong></p><p>Not all at once. Not by blindly pasting a giant configuration. One layer at a time. And this time....</p><p>if something breaks, I want to know <strong>which layer broke and why</strong>. That was the actual beginning of Round 2 (Coming Soon).</p>"},{"slug":"testing-the-best-ai-model-for-ui-in-opencode-and-somehow-ending-up-exploring-astro","t":"Testing the Best AI Model for UI in OpenCode - and Somehow Ending Up Exploring Astro","url":"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/testing-the-best-ai-model-for-ui-in-opencode-and-somehow-ending-up-exploring-astro/","author":"Amith Abey Stephen","d":"2026-09-06","tags":["Web Development"],"img":"https://pub-5911fd2c1cb64c8bb9ae3e5ffbb142b8.r2.dev/2026/09/ChatGPT-Image-Sep-3--2026--10_00_51-AM.png","mins":8,"html":"<p>I first heard about <a href=\"https://astro.new/latest/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Astro</a> from <a href=\"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/author/arjun/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Arjun Krishna</a>.<br>And honestly, if you're someone from INOVUS, there's a good chance you've heard his name before.</p><p>Arjun chettan has been around INOVUS since somewhere around 2017–18, and somehow, all these years later, he's still there — mentoring, helping, experimenting, and casually building things that make the rest of us wonder why we aren't spending more time on GitHub.</p><p>For me, he's not just another developer I know.</p><p>He's the person I genuinely look up to the most when it comes to tech. <br>His knowledge of the web is insane. But what I like even more is his curiosity. Every time something new comes out, somehow he finds a reason to try it.</p><p>And then there is his GitHub.</p><p>Bruh.</p><p>There are projects in there that genuinely look like they could be worth millions, and then you realise he built some of them just because he thought it would be fun and could learn something new!!.</p><p>Some of his hobby projects — things like Kutti-netlify (a self hosted <a href=\"https://www.netlify.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">netlify</a> with all those piplines, domain configuration..basically creating his own netlify ), Kutti-N8n (He created a <a href=\"https://n8n.io/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">n8n </a>for himself, like where you can add nodes connect nodes and execute workflows), Kutti-Lovable (basically a website generator where you have to just give in prompt and it will build website just like as we see in <a href=\"https://lovable.dev/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Lovable</a>, <a href=\"https://v0.app/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">V0</a>, <a href=\"https://bolt.new/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">bolt.new</a> etc ) and others — are exactly the kind of projects he builds for fun and learning. Well I hope, now you have clear idea of how great this man is! </p><p>So yeah, that's Arjun chettan.</p><p>But this isn't actually a blog about Arjun chettan. It's about <strong>Astro</strong>. And somehow, Once again Arjun chettan was the reason I tried up something new!</p><hr><h2 id=\"all-of-this-started-as-a-experiment-to-find-best-model-for-ui-in-opencodewith-a-prompt-for-building-portfolio\">All of this started as a experiment to find best model for UI in opencode - with a prompt for building  portfolio</h2><p>Today, me and Nithin were experimenting with different models available through <a href=\"https://opencode.ai/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><strong>OpenCode</strong>.</a></p><blockquote>OpenCode is completely free to use, without the usual limits you'd expect, and it has some pretty interesting models available.</blockquote><p>We wnated to test the free models available there to find the best model for UI designs and for that we needed something real to throw at it.</p><p>We asked for a prompt to Monson  one of our office colleague who is the designer. He gave us the prompt for his portfolio. 😂</p><p>Perfect.</p><p>We ran the same portfolio prompt through different models and compared the results.</p><p>And eventually, <a href=\"https://ai.meta.com/blog/introducing-muse-spark-meta-model-api/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Muse</a> a model by meta came out looking really good. We discussed about the result and later, I naturally did what every developer does after getting something that looks good:</p><blockquote>\"Okay... let's make it production ready.\"</blockquote><p>And that's where things got funny.</p><hr><h2 id=\"production-ready-apparently-means-everything\">\"Production ready\" apparently means EVERYTHING</h2><p>I gave Muse a very simple instruction:</p><p><strong>Make it production ready.</strong></p><p>And the model took that personally. Suddenly, my tiny portfolio project had an entire ecosystem around it.</p><p>Vercel configuration. Netlify configuration. Docker files. Prettier configuration. Various supporting files. Deployment-related setup.</p><p>Basically, the model looked at a simple project and decided:</p><p><em>\"What if we prepare this thing to be deployed literally anywhere?\"</em> 😂</p><p>At that point, the project had accumulated considerably more infrastructure than the portfolio actually needed.</p><p>So at evening I sat down with Antigravity and started cleaning things up.</p><p>Removing unnecessary files. Organising things. Making the project easier to maintain. And, most importantly, getting it into a state where <strong>Monson could actually use it as his portfolio</strong>.</p><hr><h2 id=\"then-navaneeth-gave-me-another-idea\">Then Navaneeth gave me another idea</h2><p>While discussing about whole these experinces one of our product manager, Navaneeth, said something along the lines of:</p><blockquote>\"Bro, copy my portfolio. You built it using my prompts anyway. Give it to him.\"</blockquote><p>And that reminded me of something he'd previously asked for. He wanted his portfolio to have an <strong>editor/admin page: </strong>Something where he could log in and update his portfolio content himself.</p><p>No opening the source code. <br>No asking a developer to change a heading.<br>No rebuilding the entire project just because he got a new certificate.</p><p>Just:</p><p><strong>Login → Edit → Save.</strong></p><p>And I immediately thought:</p><p><em>\"Wait... that's actually a really good idea.\"</em></p><p>So I added it.</p><hr><h2 id=\"suddenly-this-wasnt-just-a-portfolio\">Suddenly, this wasn't just a portfolio</h2><p>Now we had:</p><ul><li>A portfolio frontend</li><li>An admin/editor interface</li><li>Content management</li><li>Asset management</li><li>Cloudflare R2 for storing assets</li><li>A Cloudflare Worker handling the backend</li></ul><p>And because Muse had originally converted the project from a simple HTML implementation into React, we ended up with a React-based application.</p><p>Eventually everything was ready.</p><p>The frontend went to Vercel. <br>The backend became a Cloudflare Worker.<br>Cloudflare R2 handled the assets.</p><p>And Monson finally had a portfolio that he could actually manage himself.</p><p>Done. Right?</p><p>Nope.</p><p>Because apparently I have a problem.</p><hr>I wasn't happy with React.<p>Don't get me wrong.</p><p>I like React. I've used React a lot.</p><p>But if you ask me what framework I enjoy working with the most, my answer is probably going to be <a href=\"https://nextjs.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><strong>Next.js</strong>.</a></p><p>I love the whole monolithic experience.</p><p>Frontend. Backend. API routes. Everything in one project.</p><p>And then:</p><p><strong>Deploy → Vercel → done.</strong></p><p>No unnecessary separation. No wondering where the backend should live. No managing multiple deployments for a relatively small application.</p><p>Just one application. One repository. One deployment.</p><p>So looking at this React + Cloudflare Worker setup, I started thinking:</p><p><em>\"There has to be a nicer way to do this.\"</em></p><p>And that's when I asked Antigravity to suggest a better stack. The answer surprised me.</p><p>It basically came down to:</p><pre><code>Astro + TypeScript if I wanted maximum performance, strong design preservation and a simple architecture.\n\nNext.js + TypeScript if I wanted a heavily interactive React application with things like rich forms and server actions.</code></pre><p>And my first thought was obviously:</p><p><strong>Next.js.</strong></p><p>Because... obviously. But then I stopped.</p><p><em>\"Wait.\"</em></p><p>I've never actually tried Astro.</p><p>So why am I automatically choosing Next.js? Maybe I should finally try it. Thats how my new learning starts. Its been over 2 -3 months i heard arjun chettan mentioning it to me for 1st time , but now i too had a reaon for using it, trying it and learning how it works. What if this becomes my new favoruite, what if its much more convenient than next.js to me! </p><hr>Okay. Let's try Astro.<p>I told the model:</p><p><strong>\"I've never tried Astro. Let's go with Astro.\"</strong></p><p>In my mind, i was in hope like the model will teach e, but something else happened, under 10 sec it gave me a implementation plan</p><p>So instead of blindly letting the model do everything, I started reading the implementation plan it generated.</p><p>And Astro immediately started making more sense.</p><p>The architecture felt surprisingly familiar. Kind of structure I liked in Next.js.</p><p>You could build pages, have server-side functionality, have backend/serverless-style logic.</p><p>But none of these was the key feature of astro, it was :</p><p>Astro wasn't forcing me to commit to one UI framework.</p><p>I could use React. But I could also use Vue. or Svelte or other supported technologies. along with the other in within the same project.</p><p>That idea was fascinating. In the simplest possible way:</p><p><strong>Astro lets you build the application around Astro, while still letting you bring different UI frameworks into the parts that need them.</strong></p><p>And that flexibility was something I hadn't really explored before. I was sold. I have never properly learned to work in <a href=\"https://vuejs.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Vue.js</a>. I still remember i my exploration in vue started as part of updating the contents in our official website <a href=\"https://inovuslabs.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">inovuslabs.org</a>. Over the time on updating the texts, and navaigation through various files, minor changes out curosity teached me vue. Even now i m not well clear how to start a vue projct from scratch but ik to make way and understand a project in vue. And again thanks to arjun chettan, cause he was the one who teached me this file navigations what and why it was etc... But the twist didn't end up there, i responded proced to the gemini model for implementation and thats when another twist happend. </p><hr>And then Gemini had something to say...<p>I told the model to go ahead and start the migration. And right around then, Antigravity hit me with:</p><p><strong>NEW MODEL — GEMINI 3.8 IS HERE.</strong></p><p>Perfect timing. I switched models. 😂</p><p>And gave Gemini the job of building it. At this point, the portfolio was officially becoming an experiment. But I wasn't done yet.</p><hr>I went down the Astro rabbit hole<p>Instead of just letting the model build everything, I opened YouTube.</p><p>Searched:</p><p><strong>\"What is Astro?\"</strong></p><p>saw a 100seconds of astro video</p><figure></figure><p> then i  searched <strong>\"Getting started with Astro.\"</strong></p><p>And that's when I found a tutorial by <strong>Mike Murphy</strong>.</p><figure></figure><p>His own portfolio was built with Astro. <br><a href=\"https://mikemurphy.ai/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://mikemurphy.ai/</a></p><p>So naturally, I opened it. And honestly? It was interesting. The site the really nice color palette, and UI was simply nice!</p><p>Then I found his Astro tutorials.</p><p>And instead of just reading about Astro through an AI-generated explanation, I started going through the documentation and tutorials myself.<br><a href=\"https://mikemurphy.ai/tutorials/astro/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://mikemurphy.ai/tutorials/astro/</a> this was my reference doc along with the official docs of astro: <a href=\"https://docs.astro.build/en/getting-started/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">docs</a></p><p>I even started another small Astro project.</p><p>This time, <strong>without AI</strong>.</p><p>Because if I let AI build everything, I'll end up with a beautiful Astro project and absolutely no idea what Astro actually does.</p><p>And that's not what I want. I wanted to understand it.</p><hr>So... what started as a portfolio became something else<p>This whole thing started because we were simply testing AI models.</p><p>Then we found a model that generated a good portfolio.</p><p>Then I asked it to make it production-ready.</p><p>Then it generated half the infrastructure of the internet.</p><p>Then I cleaned it up.</p><p>Then we added an admin panel.</p><p>Then Cloudflare entered the picture.</p><p>Then I deployed the frontend to Vercel.</p><p>Then I wasn't happy with React.</p><p>Then Antigravity suggested Astro.</p><p>Then I realised I'd never actually tried Astro.</p><p>Then Gemini 3.8 conveniently showed up.</p><p>Then I went to YouTube.</p><p>Then I found Mike Murphy.</p><p>Then I started reading Astro's documentation.</p><p>And now...</p><p>I'm learning Astro.</p><p>Not because I had a task that required Astro.</p><p>Not because someone told me I needed to learn it.</p><p>But simply because I reached a point where I thought:</p><p><strong>\"I've never tried this. Why not?\"</strong></p><p>And honestly, I think that's one of the best ways to learn technology.</p><hr><h2 id=\"this-is-only-the-beginning\">This is only the beginning.</h2><p>The portfolio we're building is going to become a proper Astro project.</p><p>And alongside it, I'm building another small project completely without AI — just so I can actually understand what's happening under the hood.</p><p>In the next blog, I'll share what I actually learned while getting started with Astro:</p><p>And of course...</p><p>I'll share the finished portfolio too.</p><p><strong>Stay tuned.</strong></p><p>The portfolio experiment became an Astro experiment.</p><p>And the Astro experiment has officially begun. <br></p>"},{"slug":"the-cuda-monopoly-why-ai-developers-still-struggle-to-ditch-nvidia-in-2026","t":"The CUDA Monopoly: Why AI Developers Still Struggle to Ditch NVIDIA in 2026","url":"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/the-cuda-monopoly-why-ai-developers-still-struggle-to-ditch-nvidia-in-2026/","author":"Midhun Sujith Nair","d":"2026-08-27","tags":["Science & Technology"],"img":"https://pub-5911fd2c1cb64c8bb9ae3e5ffbb142b8.r2.dev/2026/08/watermark-removed-Gemini_Generated_Image_d0nk5sd0nk5sd0nk.png","mins":10,"html":"<p>From CUDA and cuDNN to PyTorch integrations, optimized kernels, distributed training libraries, and developer tooling, NVIDIA has built something much harder to copy than a chip: a software ecosystem that developers have spent years building on top of.</p><p>And in 2026, that ecosystem may be NVIDIA's biggest competitive advantage.</p><p>Hey! If you haven't read Part 1 yet, go check that out first so you're up to speed on the hardware and economics behind NVIDIA's AI dominance.</p><figure><a href=\"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/the-2006-c-hack-that-tricked-graphics-cards-into-powering-ai/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The 2006 C++ Hack That Tricked Graphics Cards Into Powering AI<small>We all know NVIDIA GPUs run the modern AI industry. But the real secret isn’t just their hardware, it’s a massive software gamble they took nearly 20 years ago. Welcome to Part 1 of a two-part deep dive into the software engineering that built the modern AI industry.</small></a></figure><p>In <strong>Part 2</strong>, we're going one layer deeper - into the software, libraries, and developer tooling that make NVIDIA's hardware so difficult to replace.</p><hr><h2 id=\"1-the-hardware-illusion\">1. The Hardware Illusion</h2><p>Let's get something straight.</p><p>AMD, Intel, Google, Amazon, and other companies are not incapable of building powerful AI accelerators.</p><p>AMD's Instinct accelerators, Google's TPUs, and AWS's Trainium chips all demonstrate that NVIDIA isn't the only company capable of building silicon designed for large-scale machine learning.</p><figure><img src=\"https://pub-5911fd2c1cb64c8bb9ae3e5ffbb142b8.r2.dev/2026/08/95001_03_amd-mi300x-vs-nvidia-h100-debate-heats-up-with-stating-it-has-the-performance-advantage_full.jpg\" alt=\"The CUDA Monopoly: Why AI Developers Still Struggle to Ditch NVIDIA in 2026\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption> AMD Instinct accelerators demonstrate that NVIDIA isn't the only player building high-performance hardware for modern AI workloads. It's a genuine marvel of modern semiconductor engineering. Image Source: AMD.</figcaption></figure><p>So why does NVIDIA continue to dominate so many AI workloads?</p><p>Because <strong>silicon alone doesn't run your model.</strong></p><p>A theoretical peak of hundreds of TFLOPS doesn't automatically translate into the same performance on an actual LLM.</p><p>Real-world performance depends on <strong>memory bandwidth, kernel optimization, compiler efficiency, framework support, and communication between accelerators.</strong></p><p>This is where NVIDIA's advantage starts becoming obvious.</p><p>Building a powerful accelerator is difficult.</p><p>Building an accelerator <strong>and then convincing an entire software ecosystem to optimize itself around that accelerator</strong> is considerably harder.</p><p>A fast chip without optimized software can become an expensive space heater.</p><p>And this is the first illusion we need to break:</p><blockquote>A faster chip on paper does not automatically mean a faster AI system in the real world.</blockquote><hr><h2 id=\"2-why-the-h100-is-more-than-a-gpu\">2. Why the H100 Is More Than a GPU</h2><p>Now let's look at NVIDIA's side of the equation.</p><p>If you want to understand NVIDIA's position in AI, the H100 is a good place to start.</p><p>It's tempting to look at the specifications and simply say:</p><blockquote>\"It's a really powerful GPU.\"</blockquote><p>That's missing the point.</p><p>The H100 is part of a much larger hardware and software platform designed around AI workloads.</p><figure><img src=\"https://pub-5911fd2c1cb64c8bb9ae3e5ffbb142b8.r2.dev/2026/08/hpc-press-hgx-h100-1600x900-2.jpg\" alt=\"The CUDA Monopoly: Why AI Developers Still Struggle to Ditch NVIDIA in 2026\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Image Source: NVIDIA. An NVIDIA HGX H100 platform, combining eight H100 GPUs with high-speed interconnects to form a tightly integrated AI computing system.</figcaption></figure><p>Look closely at the image.</p><p>This isn't a single graphics card sitting inside a gaming PC</p><p>You're looking at<strong> eight H100 GPUs working together as a tightly integrated accelerator platform.</strong></p><p>And that distinction matters.</p><p>Modern AI models can be so large that one accelerator isn't enough. Training and serving them efficiently often requires distributing workloads across multiple GPUs.</p><p>Once you do that, another problem appears:</p><p><strong>Communication.</strong></p><p>Imagine eight extremely powerful computers sitting in the same room. If they have to communicate through a slow connection every time they need to exchange data, much of their theoretical performance gets wasted waiting.</p><p>This is where technologies such as <a href=\"https://www.nvidia.com/en-in/data-center/nvlink/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><strong>NVLink</strong></a> become important.</p><p>NVIDIA isn't simply selling you eight individual GPUs.</p><p>It's building an ecosystem around making those GPUs communicate efficiently enough to function as part of a much larger computing system.</p><p>And then there is the silicon itself.</p><p>The H100's Hopper architecture introduced features specifically designed for modern AI workloads, including the <strong>Transformer Engine</strong>, which helps accelerate transformer-based models using lower-precision computation such as FP8.</p><p>Why does that matter?</p><p>Because modern AI isn't simply about performing more floating-point operations.</p><p>Large language models perform enormous numbers of matrix multiplications. The faster and more efficiently those operations can be performed, the more useful the accelerator becomes.</p><p>So when someone says:</p><blockquote>\"This competitor has more <a href=\"https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/computer-organization-architecture/what-is-a-teraflops/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TFLOPS.</a>\"</blockquote><p>the obvious question should be:</p><blockquote>\"Okay - but how does it perform on my actual workload?\"</blockquote><p>That's the difference between looking at a chip and looking at an AI computing platform.</p><hr><h2 id=\"3-the-real-nvidia-moat-is-the-software-stack\">3. The Real NVIDIA Moat Is the Software Stack</h2><p>Hardware integration like NVLink solves the physical communication bottleneck - but the harder problem to solve is the software that orchestrates it.</p><p>If you read Part 1, you already know the story of CUDA: NVIDIA's decision to make GPUs programmable for general-purpose computing turned out to be one of the most important bets in modern computing.</p><p><strong>But here's where the story gets interesting.</strong></p><p>CUDA didn't just make NVIDIA GPUs programmable.</p><p>Over nearly two decades, an enormous software ecosystem grew around it.</p><p>And <strong>that's the moat we're talking about.</strong></p><p>Think of the stack like this:</p><figure><img src=\"https://pub-5911fd2c1cb64c8bb9ae3e5ffbb142b8.r2.dev/2026/08/inference-social-os-ai-sw-anference-beat-5244100-v10_Slide3-1.jpg\" alt=\"The CUDA Monopoly: Why AI Developers Still Struggle to Ditch NVIDIA in 2026\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Image Source: NVIDIA</figcaption></figure><blockquote><strong>Want to see this ecosystem in action?</strong> NVIDIA's <em>Inside the NVIDIA AI Platform and Ecosystem</em> session offers a deeper look at how CUDA, libraries, frameworks, and the rest of NVIDIA's AI stack fit together.</blockquote><figure></figure><p>the important part isn't simply that these technologies exist.</p><p>It's that they've been <strong>continuously optimized together for years</strong>.</p><p>Libraries such as <a href=\"https://developer.nvidia.com/cublas\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">cuBLAS</a> and <a href=\"https://developer.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-x-libraries/cudnn\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">cuDNN</a> provide highly optimized implementations of operations that AI workloads constantly rely on.</p><p>NCCL helps with communication between GPUs.</p><p><a href=\"https://developer.nvidia.com/tensorrt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TensorRT </a>focuses on optimizing inference.</p><p>And thousands of open-source projects have been built, tested, and optimized around this ecosystem.</p><p><strong>That's the moat.</strong></p><hr><h2 id=\"4-the-pytorch-problem\">4. The PyTorch Problem</h2><p>Nobody wants to spend their weekend rewriting an entire machine-learning pipeline because they decided to use a different accelerator.</p><p>And this is where NVIDIA benefits enormously from <strong>developer familiarity</strong>.</p><p><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PyTorch\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PyTorch</a> has become one of the most widely used frameworks for modern machine learning.</p><p>And modern PyTorch is actually much more portable than it used to be.</p><p>You don't necessarily have to write NVIDIA-specific code everywhere. Instead of scattering <code>.cuda()</code> throughout your project, you can write device-independent code:</p><pre><code>device = torch.device(\"cuda\" if torch.cuda.is_available() else \"cpu\")\nmodel.to(device)</code></pre><p>That looks pretty portable, right?</p><p>So does that mean switching hardware is easy?</p><p><strong>Not necessarily.</strong></p><p>The Python code is only the top layer.</p><p>Underneath PyTorch are kernels, libraries, compilers, numerical implementations, communication primitives, and hardware-specific optimizations.</p><p>Think about the software stack we just looked at:</p><p><strong>AI Application</strong><br>\n↓<br>\n<strong>PyTorch / TensorFlow / JAX</strong><br>\n↓<br>\n<strong>CUDA / ROCm / Other Backends</strong><br>\n↓<br>\n<strong>Optimized Libraries and Kernels</strong><br>\n↓<br>\n<strong>GPU / Accelerator</strong></p>\n<p>Changing the hardware doesn't necessarily mean changing one line of Python.</p><p>It can mean changing—or at least adapting - everything underneath it.</p><h3 id=\"try-it-yourself\">Try It Yourself</h3><p>Here's a simplified simulation of what the difference can feel like.</p><p>The point isn't that alternative hardware doesn't work. It absolutely can.</p><p>The point is what happens <strong>after</strong> your model starts running.</p><p>Try both:</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n  \n    \n      ▶ Run on NVIDIA (CUDA)\n    \n\n    \n      ▶ Run on Alternative Hardware\n    \n  \n\n  \n    &gt; Waiting for execution command...\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n<p>On NVIDIA, the software stack is mature and heavily optimized around CUDA.</p><p>On alternative hardware, the model may still run perfectly well—but the level of library support, kernel optimization, compiler maturity, and hardware-specific tuning can vary depending on the workload.</p><p>And that's the distinction that often gets lost in hardware comparisons.</p><blockquote>\"It runs\" and \"it runs efficiently at scale\" are two completely different things.</blockquote><p>A model running successfully is only the beginning.</p><p>Imagine you're an AI startup with a production system built around NVIDIA GPUs.</p><p>You've got:</p><ul><li>Custom CUDA kernels</li><li>PyTorch training code</li><li>Optimized inference pipelines</li><li>Distributed training infrastructure</li><li>GPU monitoring and deployment tools</li><li>Engineers who already understand CUDA</li><li>Cloud infrastructure built around NVIDIA hardware</li></ul><p>Now another company releases an accelerator that looks better on paper.</p><blockquote>Maybe it offers more theoretical compute.</blockquote><blockquote>Maybe it has more memory bandwidth.</blockquote><blockquote>Maybe it is cheaper.</blockquote><p>Should you switch?</p><p>Maybe.</p><p>But suddenly you're not comparing <strong>GPU A vs GPU B</strong> anymore.</p><p>You're comparing:</p><p><strong>Your existing software ecosystem</strong></p><p>against</p><p><strong>the cost of migrating that ecosystem.</strong></p><p>That's the real problem.</p><p>You can technically port an application to another accelerator.</p><p>But then you may need to:</p><ul><li>Replace or adapt optimized kernels</li><li>Find equivalent libraries</li><li>Debug compatibility issues</li><li>Tune workloads for a different architecture</li><li>Rework distributed communication</li><li>Investigate unexpected performance bottlenecks</li><li>Train engineers on a different software stack</li></ul><p>And that's where NVIDIA's software moat becomes extremely valuable.</p><p>The hardware doesn't have to be impossible to replace.</p><p><strong>The accumulated engineering around the hardware does.</strong></p><p>That's why CUDA's advantage isn't simply that developers <em>like</em> it.</p><p>It's that developers, researchers, startups, cloud providers, and entire companies have spent years building infrastructure around it.</p><p>And once an ecosystem becomes this large, switching costs become a competitive advantage all by themselves.</p><hr><h2 id=\"5-why-developers-dont-just-switch\">5. Why Developers Don't Just Switch</h2><p>If the software stack makes migrating technically difficult, the operational reality makes it incredibly risky. Even when a competing accelerator looks better on paper, developers hesitate to switch for reasons that go far beyond just rewriting code.</p><h3 id=\"the-stack-overflow-effect\"><strong>The \"Stack Overflow\" Effect</strong></h3><p> When you encounter a cryptic out-of-memory error on an NVIDIA GPU, someone on the internet has already solved it. The machine learning community has spent over a decade mapping every edge case, bug, and optimization in CUDA. If you hit a compiler error on a newer, competing accelerator, you might be the first person in the world to find it. Instead of building your product, your engineers are suddenly stuck beta-testing someone else's software stack.</p><h3 id=\"hiring-and-talent\"><strong>Hiring and Talent</strong></h3><p> The industry’s default language is CUDA. When a company hires AI engineers, they are hiring people who already have muscle memory for NVIDIA's profiling tools, deployment servers, and memory management. Forcing a team to learn an entirely new ecosystem slows down development, which is a death sentence in an industry moving this fast.</p><h3 id=\"the-tflops-illusion\"><strong>The TFLOPS Illusion</strong></h3><p>This is why raw hardware benchmarks don't tell the whole story. A chip can have impressive specifications—massive memory bandwidth or sky-high theoretical compute—and still struggle to deliver on a real-world AI workload.</p><p>What matters to a developer isn't simply:</p><blockquote>\"How many TFLOPS does this accelerator have?\"</blockquote><p>It's:</p><blockquote>\"How quickly can my team build, debug, and deploy our models without hitting a wall?\"</blockquote><p>That's a much harder benchmark to win. And this is where competitors face NVIDIA's biggest problem. AMD, Intel, Google, Amazon, and others can build incredibly powerful silicon. But they aren't just competing against NVIDIA's hardware. They are competing against a decade of community trust, debugging history, and developer muscle memory.</p><hr><h2 id=\"6-could-the-cuda-moat-actually-be-broken\">6. Could the CUDA Moat Actually Be Broken?</h2><p>This doesn't mean NVIDIA is untouchable.</p><p>The interesting question is what happens if developers eventually stop caring about the hardware underneath their software.</p><p>That's where technologies such as <a href=\"https://openai.com/index/triton/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><strong>Triton</strong></a>, alternative software stacks like <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ROCm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><strong>ROCm</strong></a>, and increasingly hardware-agnostic frameworks become important.</p><p>The goal is simple:</p><blockquote><strong>Make the software portable enough that developers can choose the hardware underneath it.</strong></blockquote><p>Imagine writing an AI application without having to care whether the accelerator underneath it comes from NVIDIA, AMD, Google, or someone else.</p><p>That would completely change the competitive landscape.</p><p>Instead of asking:</p><blockquote>\"Does this accelerator support my CUDA workload?\"</blockquote><p>developers could simply ask:</p><blockquote>\"Which accelerator gives me the best performance and price for my workload?\"</blockquote><p>But there's a catch.</p><p>Different accelerators aren't identical.</p><p>They have different architectures, memory systems, instructions, communication technologies, and specialized hardware.</p><p>Making software portable is one challenge.</p><p>Making it <strong>portable while maintaining excellent performance</strong> is much harder.</p><p>And that's the real battle ahead.</p><blockquote>Portability is easy. Portable high performance is the hard part.</blockquote><p>If abstraction layers eventually become good enough, NVIDIA's software moat could weaken.</p><p>But until then, CUDA remains deeply embedded in the AI ecosystem.</p><hr><h2 id=\"conclusion-the-moat-is-software\">Conclusion: The Moat Is Software</h2><p>After almost two decades, CUDA has become much more than a programming platform.</p><p>It has become infrastructure.</p><p>Millions of developers have learned it.</p><p>Thousands of projects have been built around it.</p><p>AI frameworks integrate with it.</p><p>Libraries have been optimized for it.</p><p>Companies have built their infrastructure around it.</p><p>That's why NVIDIA's biggest competitive advantage isn't necessarily the GPU sitting inside the server.</p><p>It's everything surrounding that GPU.</p><p>The hardware can be challenged.</p><p>The software ecosystem is much harder to replace.</p><p>And that's the real CUDA monopoly.</p><p>The question isn't:</p><blockquote><strong>\"Can someone build a GPU faster than NVIDIA?\"</strong></blockquote><p>It's:</p><blockquote><strong>\"Can someone build an ecosystem that developers would rather use?\"</strong></blockquote><p>Because until that happens, replacing NVIDIA means replacing much more than a piece of silicon.</p><p>It means replacing years of software, tooling, optimization, knowledge, and developer familiarity.</p><p>And that's a much harder problem.</p><hr><h2 id=\"whats-next\">What's Next?</h2><p>This brings us to the end of our two-part journey into NVIDIA's AI dominance.</p><p>In Part 1, we looked at how NVIDIA transformed GPUs from graphics processors into the foundation of modern accelerated computing.</p><p>In Part 2, we went one layer deeper and looked at the software moat that made that hardware so difficult to replace.</p><p>The future of AI computing is still wide open.</p><p>Maybe CUDA remains dominant for another decade.</p><p>Maybe abstraction layers eventually make the underlying hardware far less important.</p><p>Or maybe the next major computing platform hasn't even been invented yet.</p><p><strong>And that's the interesting part.</strong></p><hr><h2 id=\"lets-connect\">Let's Connect!</h2><p>Is CUDA here to stay, or is the industry slowly moving toward a more hardware-agnostic future?</p><p>Share your thoughts below. I'd love to hear your perspective.</p>"},{"slug":"the-world-that-never-existed","t":"The World That Never Existed","url":"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/the-world-that-never-existed/","author":"Amith Abey Stephen","d":"2026-08-08","tags":["Free Thinking"],"img":"https://pub-5911fd2c1cb64c8bb9ae3e5ffbb142b8.r2.dev/2026/08/ChatGPT-world.png","mins":2,"html":"<p>\"Where are you?\"</p><p>That was all I asked. But the words that came back were something I never expected.</p><blockquote><em>\"Lost in the dreams of the world that never existed.\"</em></blockquote><p>For some reason, that made me want to know more.</p><p>What kind of world?  The first thing that came to my mind was probably what anyone would think. Maybe a world where I would finally be happy.</p><p>A world with the love of life.<br>A world where peace just covers me.<br>A world where I have more than enough money for all my needs.<br>A peaceful life. A life without constant problems.</p><p>That was the world I imagined.</p><p>But it wasn't.</p><blockquote><em>\"A world where expectations are less,<br>a world where this heartache never exists,<br>a world where I breathe without regret,<br>a world where this guilt stops washing over me daily.\"</em></blockquote><p>I stopped there.</p><p>Wait...</p><p>What?</p><p>Because this wasn't about wanting more. It wasn't about a perfect life. It was about wanting a life where the heart could finally rest. A place where the things happening around didn't immediately become a question:</p><p><em>\"Is it all because of me?\"</em></p><p>A place where every mistake didn't come back again and again. A place where there was no constant feeling of carrying something heavy. </p><p>All she wanted was </p><p>Validation. <br>Peace.<br>A place where she could just be happy.</p><p>I wanted to know more about that world. I wanted to understand it. But all I got was:</p><blockquote><em>\"A non-existing world is not worth discussing.\"</em></blockquote><p>And somehow, that sentence explained everything. Maybe because somewhere inside, she already believed that world was never going to happen. That the peace she wanted was too far away. That the guilt she carried was something that would never leave. That everything happening around her somehow came back to one thought:</p><p><em>\"Maybe it's all my fault.\"</em></p><p>And maybe we all have small world which we wished ever existed, Not because we wanted to leave this world, but....<br>you know it very well..</p>"},{"slug":"bro-charger-undo","t":"Bro... Charger Undo?","url":"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/bro-charger-undo/","author":"Amith Abey Stephen","d":"2026-08-08","tags":["Quality of Life","Travelogue"],"img":"https://pub-5911fd2c1cb64c8bb9ae3e5ffbb142b8.r2.dev/2026/08/stranegrimg.png","mins":5,"html":"<p>Soo like any other normal working day, yesterday also I boarded the MEMU (train)  to go to the office. There was light rain… and, the crowd was comparatively much less than every other day Due to the flood!</p><p>Yeah, anyway…This day was different.</p><p>As the train left the station and moved forward, I was standing near the door—as usual—getting all that cold air and those small raindrops coming through the door.</p><p>Idk how many of you can relate to this...but It’s one of the best feelings, bruhh. 🌧️🚆</p><p>Yh anyways...This day was different.</p><p>As train was moving in, and me enjoying the nature,  a guy from behind called me. My first thought was like…</p><p><em>“Oh shit… Is he going to tell me not to stand near the door? Or does he want me to close it?”</em></p><p>I turned around. There was this guy...</p><p>Long hair...Enough beard...Idk why... but the very first thing that came into my mind was...</p><p><em>\"Bro if u groom once... you'll actually look damn good.\"</em> 😂</p><p>I looked at him for a second...before I could think anything else...</p><p>“Bro… charger undo?”</p><p>The question was completely normal. But he was drunk. I don’t know why… I suddenly felt pity for him. I said, “No bro, I don’t have one.” And genuinely, I hadn’t taken my charger that day.<em> Allelum avashyam ulla divasam… ath edukilla.</em> 🫠</p><p>After around five minutes, he asked me again.</p><p>“Bro, charger undo? My phone is going to die.”</p><p>I again said, “No bro… I don’t have one.”</p><p>Then after some time…</p><p>“Bro, enikku charger venam.”</p><p>I was like, “Bro… njan eduthittilla.”</p><p>He was like, “Vere aarelum kaanumayirikkum alle?”</p><p>And the conversation just continued. (and i felt like bro's memory refresh interval was probably 5 minutes.)</p><p>Somewhere around Changanassery, he said:</p><p>“Njan Ernakulam povan aanu. Train edukkand aarnu… njan irangi bus pidikkam.”<br>And I don’t know… but the inner me felt relieved. Like…</p><p><em>“Okay… maybe I don’t have to handle him for the entire journey.”</em></p><p>Then he asked:</p><p>“Bro, vellam undo?”</p><p>And I was like, “No bro…” 😭<br>The last time I carried water with me was probably during my first semester of college. 🫣</p><p>After some time, again:</p><p>“Bro… enikku vellam venam.”</p><p>I said:</p><p>“Bro, ente kayyil illa. Changanassery ethemppol shops kanumm… avide irangiya madii .” (fr i just wannted to escape from him...)</p><p>He nodded. The rain continued. Changanassery reached.</p><p>But he didn’t get down.</p><p>The train started moving again. Then slowly… he started talking about himself. He said he was an assistant director. He had worked with many famous actors in the Malayalam film industry. He had been in the industry for more than 12 years. He was talking about work… about joining back after two weeks… and a few other things.</p><p>I don’t know… Hearing all that, I somehow felt even more pity for him. That's all that kept coming into my mind.</p><p>How.</p><p>He has experience. He has worked with people everyone knows. His name literally comes on Google. </p><p>Still...<br>Here he is.<br>Drunk.<br>Asking random strangers for a charger... Water... Just talking...<br>Like... how did life end up here?<br>I tried not to think much.</p><p>But then...</p><p>\"Bro... oru video edukko...\"</p><p>I took one. Then another. Then another. Then another 😭</p><p>Idkk...</p><p>Initially I thought okay...Just 10-15 more minutes... Kottayam ethumbo irangum. But then...</p><p>Bro liked my videos. 🫥</p><p>Like...Bro...Even I don't like the videos I shoot. I'm literally one of the worst people when it comes to photography.</p><p>Still bro was like...</p><p>\"Nannayittund.\" 😭😭</p><p>And he decided not to get down at kottayam, saying we can have more videos all this journey,..i was like manhh...where have i got my head into...how can i escape from this, people around thinks we both are traveling together so no ones gonna speak up for mee..!</p><p>Kottayam reached.</p><p>Again...He didn't get down. At that point...I was like...</p><p><em>\"Nahhh... I'm screwed.\"</em></p><p>He agian asked for water and i said, checkout the shops and outside and then he finally got down.</p><p>I was praying...Please... Either let the train leave before he comes back...</p><p>Or...</p><p>Please let him change his mind and go by bus. 😭</p><p>But.....<br>Bro came back, with a small juice., handed it to me.</p><p>\"Bro... ith onnu pidiche.\"</p><p>I'll be honest...That was probably the point where I crossed my patience, Not because he was drunk, Not because he asked for help, </p><p>I just wanted my peace.</p><p>Morning train...Rain...Door...Music...</p><p>That's all I wanted.</p><p>Then he again continued with his story and in middle wanted to go to restroom, w had a small argument about memu having bathrooms. The inner me was like bruhh, i m traveling in this train for past 3 years, ik it has one, don't argue with me😏</p><p>Idkkkk...I saw that as my only chance to escape and hence i badly wanted to make him use it....so i showed him and he somehow went.....</p><p>The second he went inside...</p><p>I quietly walked away to another compartment. NOT 1 or 2...i moved back 5 compartment so that by no chance i have to meet him again, i just wanted to enjoy my travel of the day cause it was very niceee.</p><p>I escaped 🙂</p><p>But...</p><p>My thoughts didn't.</p><p>Whole journey...Whole office time...Even while coming back...He was still somewhere in my head.</p><p>Idk if it was because he was drunk or because...he just looked tired.</p><p>Not physically. Life.</p><p>Maybe I'm completely wrong.<br>Maybe he has the happiest life ever.<br>Maybe yesterday was just one bad day.<br>I genuinely don't know.</p><p>But...</p><p>That whole incident reminded me of something else.</p><p>We all know someone...</p><p>Who keeps sending reels.<br>Randomly texting.<br>Trying to be in ur circle.<br>Laughing at every joke.<br>Always saying yes.<br>Always showing up.<br>Putting effort.<br>Maybe...that's just their way of saying...</p><p><em>\"Don't leave me behind.\"</em></p><p>I know people who keep everyone smiling…But somewhere inside, they know they are alone. No matter what they do.</p><p>I know people...who'd literally do anything for the people they care about...<br>Yet...when they need someone... nobody's there.</p><p>So yeah…</p><p>Yesterday wasn't really about a drunk man.It was about...how little we actually know about the people around us.</p><p>Take care of the people who put effort into being in your life. Acknowledge them.<br>Talk to them properly.<br>Be compassionate.</p><p>Because... we never really know what someone is carrying inside. 🤍</p>"},{"slug":"the-2006-c-hack-that-tricked-graphics-cards-into-powering-ai","t":"The 2006 C++ Hack That Tricked Graphics Cards Into Powering AI","url":"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/the-2006-c-hack-that-tricked-graphics-cards-into-powering-ai/","author":"Midhun Sujith Nair","d":"2026-08-07","tags":["Science & Technology"],"img":"https://pub-5911fd2c1cb64c8bb9ae3e5ffbb142b8.r2.dev/2026/08/watermark-removed-Gemini_Generated_Image_1nlgdt1nlgdt1nlg.png","mins":4,"html":"<p>I see it firsthand when I’m working at home on a big \"personal project\" of mine. Whenever I’m designing the backend to route queries between models like Llama, Mistral, and Gemma using their APIs, I realize that on the other end of those network calls, it is almost always NVIDIA hardware doing the heavy computing.</p><p>But you don't have to write low-level code for long before you realize that NVIDIA didn't win the AI war purely in the silicon fabrication labs. They won because of a wild software gamble they launched in 2006 - a platform called CUDA.</p><p>To understand why modern AI exists in its current form, you have to understand how CUDA hacked the graphics card.</p>🧠We are going to get a little technical for a second, but stick with me, the core concept is actually much simpler than it looks.<hr><h3 id=\"cpu-vs-gpu-the-core-difference\">CPU vs. GPU: The Core Difference</h3><p>To understand the problem CUDA solved, we have to look at what CPUs and GPUs actually do.</p><ul><li><strong>The CPU (Central Processing Unit):</strong> Think of a CPU like a small team of highly educated math professors. They are incredibly smart and can solve very complex, sequential logic problems exceptionally fast. But there are only a few of them (most modern computers have anywhere from 8 to 24 cores).</li><li><strong>The GPU (Graphics Processing Unit):</strong> Think of a GPU like an army of 10,000 middle schoolers doing basic arithmetic. Individually, they aren't as smart as the professors. But if you need to calculate 10,000 basic addition problems at the exact same time, the army of middle schoolers will finish instantly, while the professors are still on problem number 20.</li></ul><p>For a long time, researchers knew this massive \"army\" of parallel cores could be incredibly useful for complex math, but there was a massive barrier: GPUs only spoke \"graphics.\" Programmers had to write code in obscure graphics APIs, essentially tricking the GPU into doing math by disguising data as 3D textures.</p><hr><h3 id=\"enter-cuda-the-universal-translator\">Enter CUDA: The Universal Translator</h3><p>When NVIDIA launched CUDA in 2006, it fundamentally changed the trajectory of computer science. It allowed developers to write programs using familiar languages like C, C++, Python, and MATLAB, bypassing the graphics pipeline entirely to talk directly to the hardware.</p><figure><img src=\"https://pub-5911fd2c1cb64c8bb9ae3e5ffbb142b8.r2.dev/2026/08/nvidia--1-.jpg\" alt=\"The 2006 C++ Hack That Tricked Graphics Cards Into Powering AI\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Image Source: NVIDIA. From L to R, top to bottom: The NVIDIA Ampere GPU, MIG, Tensor Cores, RT Cores, structural sparsity, and NVLink. </figcaption></figure><p>As shown in the hardware breakdown above, modern NVIDIA GPUs are technological marvels. They feature dedicated Tensor Cores optimized specifically for the type of matrix math AI relies on. However, all of this incredible hardware is effectively useless to a developer without the software layer that tells it what to do.</p><figure><img src=\"https://pub-5911fd2c1cb64c8bb9ae3e5ffbb142b8.r2.dev/2026/08/stack-3.png\" alt=\"The 2006 C++ Hack That Tricked Graphics Cards Into Powering AI\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Image Source: NVIDIA. The CUDA software stack.</figcaption></figure><p>Looking at the software stack above, you can see why CUDA is an entire ecosystem. Popular AI frameworks like PyTorch and TensorFlow sit on top of highly optimized libraries (like cuDNN). When a developer writes a simple Python script, it is seamlessly translated down through this stack to execute on the GPU.</p><hr><h2 id=\"how-it-works-the-grid\">How It Works: The Grid</h2><p>Imagine you need to add two massive arrays of numbers together.</p><ul><li><strong>A CPU uses a serial loop.</strong> The \"<strong>professors</strong>\" march through the array one by one, solving each equation in order.</li><li><strong>With CUDA, you spawn a parallel kernel.</strong> Thousands of <strong>\"students\"</strong> start at once, each handling a single element simultaneously.</li></ul><p>Here is exactly what that difference looks like in real-time:</p>\n\n\n\n\n  \n    CPU · serial loop\n    FOR I IN 0..N\n    \n    ELEMENTS DONE · 0 / 256\n    One worker walks the array. Each element waits for the one before it. The loop is the program.\n  \n  \n  \n    GPU · parallel kernel\n    KERNEL&lt;&lt;&lt;1, N&gt;&gt;&gt;\n    \n    THREADS LAUNCHED · 0 / 256\n    256 workers start at once. Each one handles a single element. The launch is the program.\n  \n  \n  Rerun Simulation\n\n\n\n\n<hr><h2 id=\"the-ai-connection\">The AI Connection</h2><p>Deep Learning and neural networks are, at their core, just massive lists of numbers multiplying against other massive lists of numbers. When you feed a prompt into a large language model, the computer executes billions of parallel matrix multiplications.</p><p>Because NVIDIA had already built out CUDA and its associated mathematical libraries, AI researchers didn't have to build the math from scratch. They just plugged their models into CUDA. Today, with more than 20 million downloads, CUDA is the foundation that helps developers speed up high-performance applications across the globe.</p><hr><h2 id=\"0989988-further-exploration-how-the-architecture-was-built\">🎙️ Further Exploration: How the Architecture was Built</h2><p>Want to hear how this maps directly to modern AI? Check out this interview with Stephen Jones, NVIDIA's Chief CUDA Architect, where he breaks down the shift from graphics to parallel computing:</p>\n\n\n\n<hr><h2 id=\"whats-next\">What's Next?</h2><p>So, if CUDA is just a software layer, why can't competitors just build their own and dethrone NVIDIA? Why is the rest of the AI industry still locked into this specific ecosystem in 2026?</p><p>Stay tuned for <strong>Part 2: The CUDA Monopoly</strong>, where we’ll explore the PyTorch framework trap and the massive open-source rebellion trying to finally dethrone NVIDIA.</p><p>In the meantime, let's connect! Drop a comment or shoot me a message, I'd love to hear what you're working on.</p>"},{"slug":"what-is-shopify","t":"So… what actually is Shopify? 🛍️","url":"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/what-is-shopify/","author":"Amith Abey Stephen","d":"2026-08-05","tags":["Science & Technology"],"img":"https://pub-5911fd2c1cb64c8bb9ae3e5ffbb142b8.r2.dev/2026/08/ChatGPT-Image-Aug-5--2026--02_09_13-PM.png","mins":13,"html":"<p>Last time, I shared about starting my journey as a <strong>Junior Full Stack Developer</strong> and also talked about the first project I got to work on.</p><p>Since most of my work is now around Shopify, <a href=\"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/author/arjun/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Arjun Krishna</a> was actually the one who motivated me to write about what Shopify really is.</p><p>Before talking more about the projects, apps, and things I’m building, why not start from the base?</p><p><strong>So what actually is Shopify?</strong> 🤔</p><p>At first, I had a very simple understanding of it:</p><blockquote>“Shopify helps you create a website to sell your products without needing much coding or technical knowledge.”</blockquote><p>And yes… that is true.</p><p>But after exploring it more..., bruh, Shopify is much more than just a tool for creating an online store.</p><p>Let’s understand it in simple words 👇</p><p>Imagine you’re a dropshipper… or maybe you have a small shop and currently sell through Instagram or offline... You have the products ready, but now you want to sell them online.</p><p>You’ll probably need a website with </p><ul>\n<li>Product pages</li>\n<li>A shopping cart</li>\n<li>A payment system</li>\n<li>Order management</li>\n<li>Inventory tracking</li>\n<li>Customer details</li>\n<li>Discounts</li>\n<li>Shipping options</li>\n<li>A dashboard to manage everything</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Now imagine building all of this from scratch 😵‍💫. You would need developers, servers, databases, payment integrations, security systems, and a lot of technical work (when you are not a techiee)</p><p>This is where Shopify comes in.</p><p>Shopify gives you the tools and infrastructure needed to create and manage an online business without having to build the entire system yourself.</p><p>You can create your store, add your products, customize how the store looks, set prices, manage orders, accept payments, track inventory, and much more — all from one platform.</p><p>So instead of worrying about:</p><blockquote>“How do I build a secure checkout system?”</blockquote><p>or</p><blockquote>“How do I create an admin dashboard to manage orders?”</blockquote><p>a business owner can focus more on:</p><blockquote>“How do I improve my products and grow my business?”</blockquote><p>That is one of the main reasons Shopify is so useful.</p><h2 id=\"but-shopify-is-not-just-a-website-builder-099180\">But Shopify is not just a website builder 👀</h2><p>This was something I understood only after exploring it more.</p><p>Shopify does help you create an online store, but behind that store, there is a complete commerce system. For example, when a customer buys a product:</p><p>Customer visits the store<br>↓<br>Browses the products<br>↓<br>Adds a product to the cart<br>↓<br>Completes the payment<br>↓<br>An order is created<br>↓<br>The merchant receives the order<br>↓<br>The product gets packed and shipped</p><p>Shopify helps manage a large part of this entire process. It handles things like:</p><p><strong>Products</strong> — Add products, prices, images, descriptions, and different variants.<br><strong>Inventory</strong> — Keep track of how many products are available.<br><strong>Orders</strong> — View and manage customer purchases.<br><strong>Payments</strong> — Connect payment methods and process transactions.<br><strong>Customers</strong> — Store customer details and purchase history.<br><strong>Discounts</strong> — Create coupon codes, offers, and promotions.<br><strong>Analytics</strong> — Understand sales, visitors, orders, and business performance.<br><strong>Store design</strong> — Customize how the online store looks using themes.</p><p>So Shopify is not just:</p><blockquote>“A tool to make an e-commerce website.”</blockquote><p>It is more like:</p><blockquote><strong>A complete platform that helps businesses create, manage, and grow their commerce experience.</strong></blockquote><h2 id=\"okay2806-but-is-shopify-like-amazon\">Okay… but is Shopify like Amazon?</h2><p>Not exactly.</p><p>Amazon is a <strong>marketplace</strong>. If you sell through Amazon, your products are listed inside Amazon’s platform.</p><p>Shopify is different. Shopify helps you create <strong>your own branded store</strong>.<br>For example:<br>Instead of selling inside a marketplace, you can have:<br><code>yourbrand.com</code><br>The customers see your brand, your products, your design, and your experience.</p><p>Shopify can be working in the background, powering things like the products, cart, checkout, orders, and store management.</p><p>In simple words, <br>a website/ online store where the complete ui(the design) and branding and everything is those fixed by you.</p><h2 id=\"who-can-use-shopify\">Who can use Shopify?</h2><p>Shopify is not only for large companies. It is for</p><ul><li>Someone selling handmade products</li><li>A small clothing business</li><li>A local store that wants to start selling online</li><li>A creator selling digital products</li><li>A startup launching a new brand</li><li>A business selling products internationally</li><li>Large companies managing thousands of products</li></ul><p>Basically, if you have something to sell, Shopify can help you create the system needed to sell and manage it.(Not a single product, or 1 item sale like olx, but a venture or business you are trying to start!)</p><h2 id=\"not-just-this-shopify-has-a-another-facethe-dev-face-for-all-those-techies\">Not just this, Shopify has a another face - The Dev Face for all those techies..</h2><p>As a customer or business owner, you may see Shopify as a platform to create an online store, But as a developer, Shopify is much bigger. <br>Developers can build:</p>\n            \n                <h4><strong>1. Apps (public or custom)</strong></h4>\n                \n                    \n                        \n                    \n                \n            \n            <p>Extend Shopify’s core features, integrate external systems, and surface UI in admin, storefront, checkout, POS, etc.</p><p><strong>Sub-options (surfaces &amp; types)</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Admin apps &amp; tools</strong> – dashboards, workflow tools, integrations (ERP, CRM, WMS, etc.)</li><li><strong>Online store apps</strong> – theme app extensions (app blocks, app embeds), pixels</li><li><strong>Checkout apps</strong> – Checkout UI extensions, post‑purchase extensions</li><li><strong>Customer account apps</strong> – Customer Account UI extensions</li><li><strong>POS apps</strong> – POS UI extensions (smart grid tiles, carts, actions)</li><li><strong>Payments apps</strong> – custom payment providers &amp; methods</li><li><strong>Sales / custom channels</strong> – marketplace/social/mobile channels</li></ul><p><strong>Docs</strong></p><ul><li>Overview: <a href=\"https://shopify.dev/docs/apps\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://shopify.dev/docs/apps</a></li><li>App surfaces: <a href=\"https://shopify.dev/docs/apps/build/app-surfaces\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://shopify.dev/docs/apps/build/app-surfaces</a></li></ul><br>\n        \n            \n                <h4> 2. Themes &amp; Online Storefronts</h4>\n                \n                    \n                        \n                    \n                \n            \n            <p>Build or customize the Liquid-based “Online Store” channel.</p><p><strong>Sub-options</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Custom themes</strong> – full bespoke themes from scratch</li><li><strong>Theme customization</strong> – modify existing themes for merchants</li><li><strong>Theme Store themes</strong> – commercial, general‑purpose themes for the Shopify Theme Store</li><li><strong>Theme features</strong> – sections/blocks, filters, navigation, localization, markets, etc.</li></ul><p><strong>Docs</strong></p><ul><li>Theme dev overview: <a href=\"https://shopify.dev/docs/storefronts/themes\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://shopify.dev/docs/storefronts/themes</a></li><li>Theme features: <a href=\"https://shopify.dev/docs/storefronts/themes/theme-features\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://shopify.dev/docs/storefronts/themes/theme-features</a></li></ul>\n        \n            \n                <h4>3. Headless / Custom Storefronts</h4>\n                \n                    \n                        \n                    \n                \n            \n            <p>Use Shopify purely as the commerce backend, with your own frontend stack.</p><p><strong>Sub-options</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Storefront API only</strong> – any framework / language, fully custom frontend</li><li><strong>Hydrogen</strong> – Shopify’s React Router–based framework for storefronts</li><li><strong>Hydrogen React</strong> – React component/hooks library on top of Storefront API</li><li><strong>Native or PWA apps</strong> – mobile, game, AR/VR, IoT using Storefront API</li></ul><p><strong>Docs</strong></p><ul><li>Custom storefronts intro: <a href=\"https://shopify.dev/docs/storefronts/headless/getting-started\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://shopify.dev/docs/storefronts/headless/getting-started</a></li><li>Hydrogen overview: <a href=\"https://shopify.dev/docs/custom-storefronts/hydrogen\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://shopify.dev/docs/custom-storefronts/hydrogen</a></li></ul>\n        \n            \n                <h4><strong>4. Shopify Functions (backend customization)</strong></h4>\n                \n                    \n                        \n                    \n                \n            \n            <p>Customize Shopify’s backend behavior with server-side, WASM-based functions that Shopify runs for you.</p><p><strong>Sub-options</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Discount Functions</strong> – product, order, shipping discounts</li><li><strong>Shipping &amp; delivery logic</strong> – shipping discounts, delivery customizations</li><li><strong>Cart &amp; checkout validation</strong> – enforce rules on what can be purchased/how</li><li><strong>Order routing &amp; fulfillment constraints</strong></li><li><strong>Payment customizations</strong> – payment method ordering/eligibility, messaging</li></ul><p>(Almost always configured through an app + admin UI extension.)</p><p><strong>Docs</strong></p><ul><li>Functions overview: <a href=\"https://shopify.dev/docs/api/functions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://shopify.dev/docs/api/functions</a></li><li>Build discounts with Functions: <a href=\"https://shopify.dev/docs/apps/build/discounts\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://shopify.dev/docs/apps/build/discounts</a></li></ul><br>\n        \n            \n                <h4>5. Shop Minis &amp; Shop App</h4>\n                \n                    \n                        \n                    \n                \n            \n            <p>Build experiences directly inside the <strong>Shop</strong> consumer app.</p><p><strong>Sub-options</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Shop Minis</strong> – React-based mini-apps (full‑screen shopping flows) inside Shop</li><li><ul><li>Quizzes, drops, curated collections, bundles, etc.</li><li>Uses Shop Minis SDK, connects to products/cart/checkout</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Docs</strong></p><ul><li>Shop Minis: <a href=\"https://shopify.dev/docs/api/shop-minis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://shopify.dev/docs/api/shop-minis</a></li></ul>\n        \n            \n                <h4>6. Data, Analytics &amp; Automation</h4>\n                \n                    \n                        \n                    \n                \n            \n            <p>Work with store data, reporting, and automated workflows.</p><p><strong>Sub-options</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Admin API apps</strong> – sync data to external BI/warehouse, back-office systems</li><li><strong>ShopifyQL</strong> – SQL-like queries for analytics and reporting apps</li><li><strong>Webhooks &amp; integrations</strong> – react to orders, products, customers, etc.</li><li><strong>Shopify Flow integrations</strong> – custom Flow actions, triggers, templates</li></ul><p><strong>Docs</strong></p><ul><li>APIs &amp; tools overview: <a href=\"https://shopify.dev/docs/api\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://shopify.dev/docs/api</a></li><li>ShopifyQL: <a href=\"https://shopify.dev/docs/api/shopifyql\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://shopify.dev/docs/api/shopifyql</a></li><li>Flow extensions: <a href=\"https://shopify.dev/docs/apps/build/flow\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://shopify.dev/docs/apps/build/flow</a></li></ul>\n        \n            \n                <h4>7. Agentic Commerce (UCP Agents)</h4>\n                \n                    \n                        \n                    \n                \n            \n            <p>Build AI agents that can actually act on behalf of buyers across merchants (not just chat).</p><p><strong>Sub-options</strong></p><ul><li><strong>UCP agents</strong> – use Universal Commerce Protocol (UCP) and Shopify’s MCP tools to:</li><li><ul><li>Discover products across merchants</li><li>Build carts &amp; create checkouts</li><li>Hand buyers off to merchant checkout</li><li>Track orders via webhooks &amp; APIs</li></ul></li><li><strong>Universal Cart</strong> – single cart across multiple brands</li></ul><p><strong>Docs</strong></p><ul><li>Agentic commerce &amp; UCP: <a href=\"https://shopify.dev/docs/agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://shopify.dev/docs/agents</a></li></ul>\n        <p>Apps side is what I’m currently getting to explore.</p><p>Businesses use Shopify to sell. Developers use Shopify to build tools and experiences that help those businesses sell better. And that is what makes the ecosystem interesting.</p><p>A merchant might say:</p><blockquote>“I need a better way to manage my store.”</blockquote><p>A developer can build a Shopify app for that.</p><p>A merchant might say:</p><blockquote>“I want customers to discover products in a more engaging way.”</blockquote><p>A developer can create an interactive shopping experience for that.</p><p>There are options to publish it also , like the shopify app store for apps, theme store for themes similarly, hence even if a business owner doesnt come as. client but you still want to bring your ideas out, there is the space!</p><p>So Shopify is not just a platform where people create stores. It is also a large ecosystem where businesses, developers, apps, themes, and services come together.</p><h2 id=\"so-in-one-sentence2806\">So, in one sentence…</h2><blockquote><strong>Shopify helps people create and manage online businesses without having to build the entire commerce system from scratch.</strong></blockquote><p>And for developers:</p><blockquote><strong>Shopify provides an ecosystem where we can build apps, tools, integrations, and new shopping experiences for businesses and customers.</strong></blockquote><p>This is just the base!</p><h2 id=\"oke-so-how-do-you-get-started-099180\">Oke, so how do you get started 👀</h2><p>Once you decide:\n“Oke… I want to use Shopify.”\n\nThe next questions are probably: Where do I start?\n</p><ul>\n<li>Where do I start?</li>\n<li>Do I need to know coding?</li>\n<li>Is Shopify free?</li>\n<li>How much will it cost?</li>\n<li>What things do I need before creating a store?</li>\n<li>How long will it take?</li>\n<li>And what if I’m a developer and want to build using Shopify?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So instead of only talking about the concepts, let’s look at the actual things you’ll need.\n\nAnd since there are two different types of people getting started with Shopify…\nLet’s look at both.</p>\n            \n                <h4>For those who wants the website</h4>\n                \n                    \n                        \n                    \n                \n            \n            <p>I have products. I want to create a Shopify store 🛍️ for maye your small clothing brand or you sell watches… Handmade products… Digital products… Or literally anything you want to sell online.<br><br>What do you actually need?<br><br><strong>First… you need something to sell 😭</strong><br><br>This might sound obvious…But Shopify doesn’t create the business or products for you. Shopify gives you the platform to sell.<br><br>You’ll still need:</p><br><ul><li>A product or service</li><li>Product images</li><li>Product names</li><li>Product descriptions</li><li>Prices</li><li>Stock information</li><li>A way to deliver the product<br><br>For example:<br>Product:<br>Classic Silver Watch<br><br>Price:<br>₹4,999<br><br>Available:<br>20 units<br><br>Images:<br>Front view<br>Side view<br>Wrist shot<br><br>Once you have these ready, you can start creating the store by going to <a href=\"https://www.shopify.com/in\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.shopify.com/in</a> and creating in a account there.</li></ul><p>Shopify usually provides a free trial or introductory offer for new users.<br>But <strong>Shopify is not completely free forever.</strong><br><br>After the trial or promotional period, you’ll need to choose a paid plan if you want to continue running the full store with ₹1,499/mo for annual plans<br><br>For a small business, the Basic plan is usually the starting point.<br></p><p><strong>NOTE:</strong><br>The price can change based on:</p><ul><li>Your country</li><li>Shopify’s current offers</li><li>Monthly or yearly billing<br><br>So always check Shopify’s official pricing page before paying. <a href=\"https://www.shopify.com/in/pricing\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.shopify.com/in/pricing</a><br><br>But just remember: <strong>Shopify is a paid platform.</strong><br><br><strong>What will the store actually cost? 💰</strong><br><br>The Shopify subscription is only one part.<br>A basic store may have costs like:<br><br>Shopify plan:<br>Around ₹1,500–₹2,000/month<br><br>Domain: (if your want your own links like maybe a \"mystore.com\" or something..)<br>Around ₹1,000–₹2,000/year<br><br>Apps:(some are free, but most apps are subscription based)<br>₹0–₹5,000+/month<br><br>Theme:<br>Free or a one-time payment<br><br>Payment charges:<br>Depends on the payment provider<br><br>So technically, you can start with:<br><br>- A basic Shopify plan<br>- A free theme<br>- Free apps<br><br>And keep the initial cost relatively low, maybe under (₹2,499) without your own domain and under ₹5,000 with a basic domain.<br><br>Apps are for extra features, easiness of handling things etc, which is not needed on day one.<br>A lot of beginners make the mistake of installing too many apps before even getting their first customer.<br><br>Start simple.<br>Do you need coding?<br><br>Nope.<br><br>You can create a basic Shopify store without writing code.<br><br>Shopify provides:<br><br>- Ready-made themes<br>- A visual theme editor<br>- Product management<br>- Order management<br>- Store settings<br><br>You can add products and customize many parts of the store directly from Shopify Admin.<br><br>Coding becomes useful when you want:<br></li><li>A completely custom design</li><li>Special product experiences</li><li>Custom features</li><li>Advanced integrations<br><br>But it is not required to launch a basic store.<br><br><strong>What do you need before launching?</strong><br><br>Before making the store public, you should have:</li><li>Products ready</li><li>Product images</li><li>Product prices</li><li>Product descriptions</li><li>Store name</li><li>Brand logo</li><li>Payment setup</li><li>Shipping setup</li><li>Domain</li><li>Contact information</li><li>Refund policy</li><li>Privacy policy</li><li>Terms and conditions</li></ul><p>And most importantly…<br><strong>Test the full customer journey.</strong><br><br>Open the store on your phone.<br>Browse products.<br>Add something to the cart.<br>Go through checkout.<br>Make sure everything works.<br><br>Because the store might look good on your laptop but be broken on mobile😭</p>\n        \n            \n                <h4>Those techies who wants to get started</h4>\n                \n                    \n                        \n                    \n                \n            \n            <p>Now the developer path is different.<br><br>You don’t need products...You don’t need to start a real business...And you don’t need to pay for a Shopify store just to learn development.<br><br>Shopify provides tools for developers to create development stores and build Shopify apps.</p><p>Get Started :</p><p><strong>Phase 1 — Set up your Shopify dev environment</strong><br><strong>1. Create a Shopify Partner account (free)</strong><br>This is your main developer hub: apps, dev stores, billing, etc.</p><ul><li>Go to: <a href=\"https://partners.shopify.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://partners.shopify.com</a></li><li>Sign up and complete the basics (business name can be “Personal” if needed).</li></ul><p><strong>2. Create a development store</strong><br>A dev store is a free, safe playground for coding and testing.</p><ul><li>In Partner Dashboard → <strong>Stores</strong> → <strong>Add store</strong> → choose <strong>Development store</strong>.</li><li>Turn on test data (products, customers) so you have something to work with.</li></ul><p><strong>3. Install Shopify CLI</strong><br>The CLI is how you scaffold apps, themes, and Hydrogen storefronts.</p><ul><li>Install (Node 18+ or 20+ recommended):</li></ul><p>``` npm i -g @shopify/cli@latest ```</p><ul><li>Docs: <a href=\"https://shopify.dev/docs\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Shopify CLI overview</a></li></ul><br><p><strong>Phase 2 — Pick your first track (app or theme)</strong></p><p>Pick one to start. You can always learn the others later.</p><p>Option A: Start with an <strong>App</strong> (recommended if you like backend, React, integrations)</p><p><strong>4A. Scaffold your first app</strong></p><p>From any folder:</p><p>``` shopify app init<br> cd &lt;your-app-folder&gt;<br> shopify app dev```</p><p>This will:</p><ul><li>Log you into your Partner account</li><li>Create the app in your Dev Dashboard</li><li>Connect it to your dev store</li><li>Start a local dev server and tunnel</li></ul><p>Open the URL it prints and <strong>install the app</strong> on your dev store.</p><p>Docs: <a href=\"https://shopify.dev/docs/apps/build/scaffold-app\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Scaffold an app</a></p><p><strong>5A. Explore the basic app code</strong></p><p>Out of the box you’ll get:</p><ul><li>A React Router–based embedded admin UI</li><li>API routes for calling the Admin API</li><li>Example code (e.g., “Generate a product”)</li></ul><p>Spend a bit of time to understand:</p><ul><li>Where the UI lives (usually <code>app/routes/*</code> or <code>web/frontend</code>)</li><li>Where backend logic/API calls live (e.g., <code>app/routes/api.*</code>, <code>web/backend</code>)</li></ul><p><strong>6A. Build a tiny real feature</strong></p><p>Examples of first features:</p><ul><li>A page that lists products from the Admin API</li><li>A button that tags all products in a collection</li><li>A simple “note” field on orders stored in your own DB</li></ul><p>Use the dev server while testing:</p><p>``` shopify app dev ```</p><p>When stable, commit to Git.</p><p><strong>7A. Learn how you’d ship it (even if you don’t yet)</strong></p><p>You don’t need to publish on day 1, but understand the paths:</p><ul><li><strong>Public app</strong> → listed in the <strong>Shopify App Store</strong></li><li><strong>Custom app</strong> → installed by direct link for a single client/store</li></ul><p>Docs:</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://shopify.dev/docs/apps/launch/distribution\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">App distribution overview</a></li></ul><br><p><strong>Option B: Start with a Theme (if you like Liquid, HTML/CSS, storefront UI)</strong></p><p><strong>4B. Create your first theme project</strong></p><p>From any folder:</p><p>``` <br> shopify theme init<br> cd &lt;your-theme-folder&gt;<br> shopify theme dev --store &lt;your-dev-store.myshopify.com&gt;<br>```</p><p>You’ll clone a starter theme and start a local dev server with live preview.</p><p>Docs:</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://shopify.dev/docs/storefronts/themes/getting-started/create\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Create a theme</a></li><li><a href=\"https://shopify.dev/docs/storefronts/themes/tools/cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CLI for themes</a></li></ul><p><strong>5B. Learn the basics of Liquid &amp; theme structure</strong></p><p>Focus on:</p><ul><li><code>layout/</code>, <code>templates/</code>, <code>sections/</code>, <code>snippets/</code></li><li>How sections and blocks are configured via JSON schema</li><li>How to output dynamic data with Liquid, e.g.:</li></ul><p>liquidCopy1234 {{ product.title }} {% for product in collections.frontpage.products %} {{ product.title }} {% endfor %}</p><p><strong>6B. Make 2–3 visible changes</strong></p><p>For example:</p><ul><li>Add a new section to the homepage (e.g., custom banner or featured products)</li><li>Add a setting to an existing section (custom text or image)</li><li>Change product page layout using an extra snippet</li></ul><p>Preview changes with:</p><p>bashCopy1shopify theme dev</p><p><strong>7B. Understand how themes are “shipped”</strong></p><ul><li>For clients: upload/push theme to their store, publish as live theme.</li><li>For Theme Store: later, you can package and submit for review.</li></ul><p>Docs: <a href=\"https://shopify.dev/docs/storefronts/themes\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Theme dev overview</a></p><br><p><strong>Phase 3 — Learn APIs &amp; Extensions</strong></p><p>Once you’re comfortable with a basic app or theme:</p><p><strong>8. Learn the Admin and Storefront APIs</strong></p><ul><li>Admin API: build tools/integrations for merchants (products, orders, customers).</li><li>Storefront API: power custom storefronts, mobile apps, headless.</li></ul><p>Docs: <a href=\"https://shopify.dev/docs/api\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Shopify APIs, libraries, and tools</a></p><p><strong>9. Try one “extension surface”</strong></p><p>Pick <em>one</em> of these to play with:</p><ul><li><strong>Theme app extension</strong> (if you started with apps + storefront):<br>Add an app block into the Online Store.</li><li><strong>Checkout UI extension</strong>:<br>Put a small banner or line item block in checkout.</li><li><strong>Customer Account UI extension</strong>:<br>Add a panel to the new customer accounts.</li></ul><p>Each has a CLI template:</p><p>``` shopify app generate extension ```</p><p>Docs (entry point): <a href=\"https://shopify.dev/docs/apps/build/app-surfaces\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">App surfaces</a><br><br>And that is the interesting part…<br><br>As a merchant, Shopify helps you sell.<br><br>As a developer, Shopify gives you the tools to build products that help merchants sell better.</p>\n        <p>Soo… which one are you? 👀\n\nIf you have products and want to sell:</p><ul>\n<li>Start with Shopify Admin, products, themes, payments and shipping.</li>\n<li>Your starting cost will usually include:<br>\nShopify plan + domain + any apps or services you need.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If you’re a developer:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Start with a Shopify Partner account and a development store.»</li>\n<li>Your initial cost can be: ₹0.</li>\n<li>And then choose what you want to build: Themes, Apps, Custom storefronts, Shopping experiences (shop mini)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Soo yh…\n\nGetting started with Shopify doesn’t mean the same thing for everyone.</p><p>For a business owner, it means:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>“How do I launch and run my online store?”</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For a developer, it means:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>“How do I build things inside the Shopify ecosystem?”</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>\nTwo different paths… Two different requirements… But both can start today 👀\nAnd this is just the practical starting point.\n</p>"},{"slug":"ai-is-becoming-our-second-brain-but-dont-let-it-become-your-only-one","t":"AI is becoming our second brain… but don't let it become your only one.","url":"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/ai-is-becoming-our-second-brain-but-dont-let-it-become-your-only-one/","author":"Amith Abey Stephen","d":"2026-07-17","tags":[],"img":"https://pub-5911fd2c1cb64c8bb9ae3e5ffbb142b8.r2.dev/2026/07/ChatGPT-Image-Jul-17--2026--11_27_05-AM.png","mins":4,"html":"<p>AI is everywhere now.</p><p>Need to write an email? AI.<br>Need to debug a bug? AI.<br>Need a regex? AI.<br>Need to think? ...AI.</p><p>And honestly?<br>That's not necessarily a bad thing.</p><p>We now have access to better answers, faster learning, and knowledge that would've taken hours to find a few years ago. It has genuinely made us better developers, designers, writers... basically everything.</p><p>But...</p><p>There's one thing I've been noticing lately. We jump to AI way too quickly.</p><p>I still remember teachers saying...</p><blockquote>\"Use your brain. It must've rusted.\"</blockquote><p>Back then we used to laugh. Now... it kinda hits differently. 😂</p><p>Before even giving ourselves 5 minutes to think, we open ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, Cursor... whichever AI is closest.</p><p>Sometimes... just spending those few minutes thinking about the problem yourself makes a huge difference.</p><p>Not because AI is bad.</p><p>But because <strong>thinking is a skill.</strong> And skills disappear if we stop using them. AI should help your thinking... Not replace it.</p><hr><h2 id=\"something-i-realized-while-coding\">Something I realized while coding...</h2><p>I'm a developer.<br>So naturally I use AI every single day.</p><p>Cursor.<br>Claude.<br>ChatGPT.<br>GitHub Copilot.</p><p>They're amazing.</p><p>But they also made me realize something...</p><p>Sometimes AI generates a huge chunk of code...<br>And without even reading it...<br>I just click...</p><p><strong>Allow.</strong></p><p>That's a pretty big red flag.<br>We trust AI so much that we forget we're the one shipping the code.<br>Before clicking <strong>Accept</strong> or <strong>Allow</strong>...</p><p>Read it.<br>Understand what changed.<br>Ask yourself...</p><ul><li>Why did it change this?</li><li>Is this actually solving my problem?</li><li>Did it modify something unrelated?</li><li>Could this introduce a security issue?</li></ul><p>Because once that code reaches production...<br>It's your code.<br>Not the AI's.</p><hr><h2 id=\"the-bigger-issue-nobody-talks-about-enough\">The bigger issue nobody talks about enough...</h2><p>Debugging.</p><p>We've all done this.</p><p>The app isn't working. <br>The API is failing. <br>You're frustrated.</p><p>So you copy...</p><p>The logs.<br>The config.<br>The <code>.env</code>.<br>The request headers.<br>The tokens.<br>The API keys.<br>The database URL.</p><p><strong>Everything...</strong></p><p>Ctrl + C.<br>Ctrl + V.</p><p>\"Hey ChatGPT... why isn't this working?\"</p><p>Yeah...I've done it too.</p><p>And that's isn't safe. That practice is not at all good!</p><hr><h2 id=\"my-own-mistake\">My own mistake.</h2><p>A few weeks ago I was setting up WireGuard between my three servers.<br>I honestly didn't know enough about it. And instead of spending time understanding how it worked...</p><p>I just followed AI.<br>Something wasn't working.</p><p>So I copied almost everything into ChatGPT.<br>Configs.<br>Logs.<br>Keys.<br>Basically... everything.</p><p>I knew what I'd done is not the right thing, but after getting my issue fixed,</p><p>The first thing I did?</p><p>I rotated every single key.<br>Revoked the old ones.<br>Generated new ones.<br>Problem solved.</p><p>But it made me think...</p><p>What if I never did it? Never rotated my keys, making my own servers vulnerable and thinking of all the worst case scenarios.</p><hr><h2 id=\"its-just-ai-itll-be-fine\">\"It's just AI... it'll be fine.\"</h2><p>Maybe.<br>Maybe not.<br>Most AI providers work hard to protect user data.</p><p>But once you've pasted a secret somewhere, you've already lost control over where that secret has been copied, cached, logged, or shared within your own workflows.</p><p>There are chances that the AI itself exposes it as example to someone else going through the same, the habit of casually sharing secrets is dangerous.</p><p>This gives him a direct access to my server and basicaly he could do anything with my servers 💀💀</p><p>An API key is an API key.<br>A production database password is still a production database password.<br>A private SSH key is still a private SSH key.<br><strong>Treat them like passwords.</strong><br>Because they are.</p><hr><h2 id=\"some-simple-habits-every-developer-should-follow\">Some simple habits every developer should follow</h2><p>Nothing fancy.</p><p>Just a few things that save a lot of pain later.</p><p>✅ Never paste production API keys into AI.<br>✅ Never share private keys, SSH keys, WireGuard configs, or database passwords.<br>✅ If you accidentally shared one...</p><p>Rotate it immediately.<br>Revoke the old key.<br>Generate a new one.<br>Don't wait.</p><p>✅ Before pushing a project public...</p><p>Check your repository.<br>Make sure there isn't a <code>.env</code> file hiding somewhere.<br>No secrets.<br>No tokens.<br>No credentials.<br>Use tools like <code>.gitignore</code>, environment variables, and secret scanners.</p><p>✅ And finally...</p><p>Don't blindly click <strong>Accept</strong> on AI-generated code.<br>Understand it first.</p><hr><h2 id=\"ai-is-a-tool\">AI is a tool.</h2><p>Not a replacement.<br>Not a brain transplant.<br>The best developers won't be the ones who ask AI the most questions.<br>They'll be the ones who know <strong>which questions to ask</strong>, <strong>when to trust the answer</strong>, and <strong>when to stop and think for themselves.</strong><br>AI can write code.<br>But you're still responsible for shipping it.<br>AI can suggest solutions.<br>But you're still responsible for understanding them.<br>AI can help build products.<br>But you're still responsible for keeping them secure.</p><hr><p>So yeah...<br>Keep using AI.<br>I definitely will.<br>Just...<br>Think first.<br>Read before clicking <strong>Allow</strong>.<br>And please...<br>Don't paste your production secrets into a chatbot.</p>"},{"slug":"build-your-own-vpn","t":"Stop Giving Your Data to Strangers: Build Your Own Virtual Private Network","url":"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/build-your-own-vpn/","author":"Naveen Joseph","d":"2026-07-12","tags":["Science & Technology"],"img":"https://pub-5911fd2c1cb64c8bb9ae3e5ffbb142b8.r2.dev/2026/07/wgvpn.png","mins":9,"html":"<p>My brother is my hero in my life and in this blog. Many people think IT jobs are just copy-paste work, but now it's evolved—just prompt AI and it'll do everything, right? Don't worry, you'll see AI's work in this blog because honestly, it explains things better than I do. But let me get back to my brother. He knows IT jobs are tricky, but he still teases me about the whole \"copy-paste\" thing. That's exactly why I decided to build something crazy for his daily life—to prove there's more to it than meets the eye.</p><p>My brother lives in Dubai, and whenever he wants to video call with us back home or catch up on his favorite shows, he needs a VPN. Like most people, he started with those \"totally trustworthy\" free VPN apps—you know, the ones with 5-star reviews that are probably written by bots. But here's the scary truth: <strong>using random VPN services is like giving your house keys to a stranger and hoping they won't rob you.</strong> And here's the kicker—there's no use for authentication and authorization in some situations because these technologies are helpless when the VPN provider itself is the threat. These free VPNs will log and sell your browsing data to advertisers, inject their own ads into your web traffic, steal your passwords and sensitive information, use your device as an exit node for other users' traffic (making YOU liable for their activities!), and some even contain malware or spyware as a bonus gift. It's basically digital Russian roulette where every chamber is loaded.</p><p>That's when I decided to set up a proper VPN solution for him using WireGuard. No sketchy third parties, no data logging, just secure, encrypted communication between family members. And you know what? It was surprisingly easy! Since its introduction in 2016, WireGuard has become the gold standard for VPN technology, forming the foundation for next-generation solutions like Tailscale and Headscale. So what makes WireGuard so special, and why should you ditch those random VPN apps and set up your own secure tunnel?</p><p>I spent some time researching different VPN solutions because I'm an open-source fanboy. I looked at OpenVPN, IPsec, and a bunch of other options. OpenVPN has over 100,000 lines of code, which is great if you love complexity and debugging nightmares. Then I found WireGuard, and it was like someone finally asked, \"What if we just... didn't make this so complicated?\"</p><p>Created by Jason A. Donenfeld (seriously, this guy deserves a medal), WireGuard is what happens when you apply the KISS principle—Keep It Simple, Stupid—to VPN technology. With just around 4,000 lines of code, it does everything OpenVPN does but embarrassingly faster, way more secure (smaller code means fewer bugs and vulnerabilities), and so much easier to set up that you don't need a PhD to figure it out. In 2020, even Linux was impressed enough to include it in the kernel (version 5.6). That's like getting a permanent seat at the cool kids' table. Now it's everywhere—your phone, your router, your server, probably your smart fridge soon.</p><p>But let's talk real numbers here. WireGuard is 2-3x faster than OpenVPN, takes 5 minutes to set up instead of 30+ minutes, and your config file is 10 lines instead of 50+. NAT traversal? Built-in. Learning curve? Actually manageable. OpenVPN is like using a flip phone when smartphones exist—sure, it technically works, but why would you torture yourself? WireGuard is the VPN protocol that makes other VPN protocols look like they're stuck in 2005. 🚀</p><h3 id=\"quick-config-comparison\">Quick Config Comparison</h3><p><strong>WireGuard</strong> (10 lines, done!):</p><pre><code>[Interface]\nPrivateKey = &lt;key&gt;\nAddress = 10.0.0.1/24\nListenPort = 51820\n\n[Peer]\nPublicKey = &lt;peer-key&gt;\nAllowedIPs = 10.0.0.2/32\nEndpoint = peer.example.com:51820\n</code></pre><p><strong>OpenVPN</strong> (50+ lines, plus certificate hell):</p><pre><code>dev tun\nproto udp\nremote vpn.example.com 1194\nca ca.crt\ncert client.crt\nkey client.key\ncipher AES-256-CBC\nauth SHA256\ncomp-lzo\n# ... and 40 more lines you'll never understand\n</code></pre><p>Oh, and if you're stuck with Cisco AnyConnect at work, you know the pain—slow as molasses, huge client software that eats hundreds of MBs, requires admin rights to install, constant connection drops, and expensive licensing. WireGuard is the complete opposite—lightweight, fast, and free. Your IT department might not know about it yet, but they should! 😉</p><h2 id=\"why-build-your-own-vpn-instead-of-using-tailscale\">Why Build Your Own VPN Instead of Using Tailscale?</h2><p>You've probably heard about Tailscale and Headscale. They're basically WireGuard with a nice UI and automatic setup. But here's the catch—Tailscale puts a coordination server between your devices, meaning your connection info goes through their servers. If their service goes down, so does your VPN. Plus, you're stuck with their pricing. Headscale lets you self-host, but it's still extra work.</p><p>I prefer building from scratch. It's actually free (unlimited devices vs Tailscale's 20-device limit), you learn how VPNs actually work, you get total control over routing and firewall rules, and maximum privacy—no coordination servers, no third parties, no \"trust us\" promises. Just you, your server, and encrypted goodness. Perfect for connecting family (like my brother in Dubai), linking offices, securing Docker containers, accessing IoT devices, or just having a learning lab.</p><h2 id=\"quick-start-setting-up-wireguard-from-scratch\">Quick Start: Setting Up WireGuard from Scratch</h2><h3 id=\"server-setup\">Server Setup</h3><pre><code># Install WireGuard\nsudo apt update\nsudo apt install wireguard\n\n# Generate keys\nwg genkey | tee privatekey | wg pubkey &gt; publickey\n\n# Create configuration\nsudo nano /etc/wireguard/wg0.conf\n</code></pre><p><strong>Server Configuration:</strong></p><pre><code>[Interface]\nPrivateKey = &lt;server-private-key&gt;\nAddress = 10.0.0.1/24\nListenPort = 51820\nPostUp = iptables -A FORWARD -i wg0 -j ACCEPT; iptables -t nat -A POSTROUTING -o eth0 -j MASQUERADE\nPostDown = iptables -D FORWARD -i wg0 -j ACCEPT; iptables -t nat -D POSTROUTING -o eth0 -j MASQUERADE\n\n[Peer]\nPublicKey = &lt;client-public-key&gt;\nAllowedIPs = 10.0.0.2/32\n</code></pre><h3 id=\"client-setup\">Client Setup</h3><p><strong>Client Configuration:</strong></p><pre><code>[Interface]\nPrivateKey = &lt;client-private-key&gt;\nAddress = 10.0.0.2/24\nDNS = 1.1.1.1\n\n[Peer]\nPublicKey = &lt;server-public-key&gt;\nEndpoint = server.example.com:51820\nAllowedIPs = 0.0.0.0/0\nPersistentKeepalive = 25\n</code></pre><h3 id=\"start-the-vpn\">Start the VPN</h3><pre><code># Server\nsudo wg-quick up wg0\nsudo systemctl enable wg-quick@wg0\n\n# Client\nsudo wg-quick up wg0\n</code></pre><h2 id=\"security-what-if-someone-gets-your-keys-099490\">Security: What If Someone Gets Your Keys? 🔐</h2><p>I hope by now you have an idea about me and that I'm serious about security. You've also seen what I've done in my homelab. So let's talk about the elephant in the room—what happens if your private key gets compromised? The honest answer: you're screwed. But here's how to minimize the damage.</p><p>Store your keys on an encrypted disk or partition, keep backups in a password manager, or use a hardware security module if you're fancy. Never—and I mean never—store them in plain text files in your home directory, commit them to Git repositories (even private ones!), or send them to yourself via Slack or Discord. If your key does get compromised, immediately generate new keys, update all peer configurations, restart WireGuard on all devices, check logs for suspicious activity, and consider changing your VPN subnet. Set up key rotation every 3-6 months. Future you will thank present you.</p><p>Here's a quick key rotation script to make your life easier:</p><pre><code>#!/bin/bash\n# Generate new keys\nwg genkey | tee new_privatekey | wg pubkey &gt; new_publickey\n\n# Backup old keys (just in case)\ncp /etc/wireguard/wg0.conf /etc/wireguard/wg0.conf.backup\n\n# Update config with new keys\n# (You'll need to do this manually or script it)\n\necho \"New keys generated! Update your configs and restart WireGuard.\"\n</code></pre><h2 id=\"common-issues-youll-face\">Common Issues You'll Face</h2><p>Let's be real—things will break. Here are the usual suspects. First, the firewall. Spent 2 hours debugging only to realize the firewall was blocking everything? Classic. Run <code>sudo ufw allow 51820/udp</code> and save yourself the headache. Always check the firewall first. It's ALWAYS the firewall.</p><p>Second, IP forwarding. VPN connects but can't access anything? You forgot to enable IP forwarding. Run <code>echo \"net.ipv4.ip_forward=1\" | sudo tee -a /etc/sysctl.conf</code> and <code>sudo sysctl -p</code>. Check with <code>cat /proc/sys/net/ipv4/ip_forward</code>—it should return <code>1</code>, not <code>0</code>.</p><p>Third, DNS leaks. Your VPN is working, but DNS requests are still going through your ISP. Add <code>DNS = 1.1.1.1</code> to your WireGuard config and test for leaks at <a href=\"https://dnsleaktest.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://dnsleaktest.com/</a>. Don't trust, verify!</p><p>Fourth, MTU problems. Everything connects but performance is terrible? Packets are too big for the tunnel. Add <code>MTU = 1420</code> to your interface config. If that doesn't work, try 1280.</p><p>Fifth, the \"it worked yesterday\" mystery. Common culprits: ISP changed your IP (dynamic IP strikes again!), router rebooted and forgot port forwarding, keys expired, or server ran out of disk space. Your best friend for troubleshooting: <code>sudo wg show</code>.</p><p>For monitoring, use <code>sudo wg show</code> to check status, <code>sudo wg show wg0</code> for active connections, and <code>sudo wg show wg0 transfer</code> for bandwidth. Backup your config files, private keys (encrypted!), network topology, and firewall rules. Store them on an encrypted USB drive, password manager, or encrypted cloud storage. Test your backups—can you restore from scratch? Try it before you need it.</p><h2 id=\"the-future-of-wireguard\">The Future of WireGuard</h2><p>WireGuard's adoption continues to grow:</p><ul><li><strong>Native support</strong> in major operating systems (Linux, Windows, macOS, iOS, Android)</li><li><strong>Enterprise adoption</strong> by major cloud providers (AWS, Azure, GCP)</li><li><strong>Router integration</strong> in pfSense, OPNsense, OpenWrt</li><li><strong>Continued development</strong> of user-friendly tools and interfaces</li></ul><h2 id=\"conclusion-lets-be-honest-about-the-elephant-in-the-room-099098\">Conclusion: Let's Be Honest About the Elephant in the Room 🐘</h2><p><strong>\"But wait... don't I need a static IP for this?\"</strong></p><p>YES! And if I told you that in the prerequisites, you'd probably skip this entire blog. 😅</p><p>Here's the reality: Traditional WireGuard setup needs a static IP or domain name for the server. But here's the thing—<strong>there are solutions, and I want YOUR input!</strong></p><h3 id=\"the-static-ip-challenge\">The Static IP Challenge</h3><p>Most home internet connections have dynamic IPs that change randomly. This breaks your VPN connection. The \"easy\" solutions involve middlemen:</p><ul><li>Dynamic DNS services (DuckDNS, No-IP) ← Still a middleman!</li><li>Cloudflare tunnels ← Middleman alert!</li><li>VPS with static IP ← Costs money, but at least YOU control it</li></ul><h3 id=\"my-solution-vs-your-solution\">My Solution vs Your Solution</h3><p>I've figured out a way to handle this <strong>without any middleman services</strong>—keeping everything under our control. But I'm not going to claim it's the only way or the best way.</p><p><strong>Here's the deal:</strong></p><p>🤔 <strong>Think about it first:</strong> What's YOUR solution to the dynamic IP problem? Can you solve it without introducing a middleman?</p><p>💡 <strong>Share your solution:</strong> If you've got a better approach than mine, I want to hear it! Drop it in the comments or ping me.</p><p>🤝 <strong>Struggling with this?</strong> Hit me up, and I'll share my solution. But I want you to think about it first—that's how we learn!</p><h3 id=\"the-bottom-line\">The Bottom Line</h3><p>Setting up WireGuard from scratch means:</p><ul><li>✅ <strong>Complete control</strong> over your data</li><li>✅ <strong>No middleman</strong> logging your connections</li><li>✅ <strong>Zero monthly costs</strong> (after initial setup)</li><li>✅ <strong>Learning</strong> how things actually work</li><li>⚠️ <strong>One challenge:</strong> The static IP problem (but it's solvable!)</li></ul><p><strong>Don't let the static IP thing scare you.</strong> Every problem has a solution, and the best solutions come from thinking it through yourself before Googling or asking ChatGPT.</p><p><strong>Ready to set this up this weekend?</strong> You can have a working WireGuard VPN in 30 minutes (once you solve the IP challenge). Your privacy will thank you. And you'll actually understand how your VPN works.</p><p>Now go build something awesome! 🚀</p><h2 id=\"resources\">Resources</h2><ul><li><strong>Official WireGuard Website:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.wireguard.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.wireguard.com/</a></li><li><strong>WireGuard Documentation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.wireguard.com/quickstart/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.wireguard.com/quickstart/</a></li><li><strong>Tailscale:</strong> <a href=\"https://tailscale.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://tailscale.com/</a></li><li><strong>Headscale:</strong> <a href=\"https://github.com/juanfont/headscale\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://github.com/juanfont/headscale</a></li><li><strong>WireGuard GitHub:</strong> <a href=\"https://github.com/WireGuard\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://github.com/WireGuard</a></li></ul><hr><p><em>Have you set up WireGuard in your infrastructure? Share your experiences and use cases in the comments below!</em></p>"},{"slug":"the-way-we-discover-products-is-changing","t":"The Way We Discover Products is Changing","url":"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/the-way-we-discover-products-is-changing/","author":"Amith Abey Stephen","d":"2026-07-11","tags":["Science & Technology","Web Development"],"img":"https://pub-5911fd2c1cb64c8bb9ae3e5ffbb142b8.r2.dev/2026/07/ChatGPT-Image-Jul-11--2026--03_07_05-PM.png","mins":3,"html":"<p>It's been almost a month since I started working. Honestly... life changed a lot. A month back I was just another college guy. College, INOVUS, random ideas, side projects, late night coding... that was my routine.</p><p>Now life is more like... Sleep. Travel. Office. Travel back. Some me time. Sleep. And repeat.</p><p>The sudden change in environment, the people around me, the work culture, the schedule... everything felt weird at first. I'm still getting used to it.<br>One thing I noticed is that I haven't really spent much time on my own projects this month. Initially I felt bad about it because building my own stuff is something I genuinely enjoy.</p><p>But...</p><p>At work, we built something really cool.<br>And while building it, I ended up learning something that completely changed the way I look at the web.</p><p>We all shop, right? </p><p>For years, the way we shopped online was almost the same. We'd open a shopping website, search for a product, apply filters, compare products, read reviews, maybe watch a few YouTube videos, and finally buy it. That was the normal flow.</p><p>But now...</p><p>Think about how many times you've opened ChatGPT before opening Amazon. You don't search anymore... You ask.</p><blockquote><em>\"Suggest me the best mechanical keyboard for programming.\"<br>\"What's the best monitor under ₹20k?\"<br>\"Recommend a good skincare routine for oily skin.\"</em></blockquote><p>Instead of giving you hundreds of products, AI understands what you're actually looking for and recommends a few that match your needs.</p><p>That made me think...</p><p><strong>How does AI decide which product to recommend?</strong></p><p>If my product exists...<br>Why would AI recommend mine instead of someone else's?</p><p>For years we were building websites for search engines.</p><p>SEO was everything.<br>Keywords. Meta tags. Backlinks. Search rankings.<br>The goal was simple...<br>Rank higher.<br>But AI doesn't really think like a search engine.</p><p>Earlier people searched with keywords. Now they ask questions.</p><p>Earlier someone would search...</p><p><em>\"wireless keyboard\"</em></p><p>Now it's more like...</p><p><em>\"I'm a software developer. I code for almost 8 hours a day. I need a comfortable wireless keyboard under ₹5,000. Which one should I buy?\"</em></p><p>See the difference?</p><p>That isn't just a keyword anymore. <br>That's context. <br>That's intent. <br>And AI understands that.</p><p>That's where I came across terms like <strong>Generative Engine Optimization (GEO)</strong> and <strong>Answer Engine Optimization (AEO).</strong></p><p>Instead of only optimizing websites for Google...<br>We're now optimizing websites for AI. Things like proper sitemaps, structured content, FAQs that answer real questions, schema markup, and even files like <strong>llms.txt</strong> help AI understand what your website is actually about.</p><p>It's not about stuffing keywords anymore. It's about giving AI enough context to confidently answer questions using your content.</p><p>Another thing I found really interesting was <strong>Agentic Readiness</strong>.</p><p>We're slowly moving into a world where AI won't just answer questions. It'll compare products. Browse websites. Book services. Maybe even purchase things on our behalf.</p><p>Which means...</p><p>Your website isn't just being visited by humans anymore. It's also being visited by AI. And if AI doesn't understand your product... How will it recommend it?</p><p>I don't think SEO is going away.</p><p>Not at all.</p><p>But I do think we're entering a phase where SEO alone isn't enough.<br>The way people discover products is changing.<br>From keywords to conversations.<br>From search engines to answer engines.<br>From humans browsing websites to AI recommending them.</p><p>If you're building a product, a website, or even an online store...<br>Maybe the question isn't just,</p><p><strong>\"Will people find my product?\"</strong></p><p>Maybe it's...</p><p><strong>\"Will AI recommend my product when someone asks for it?\"</strong></p><p>I'd love to hear what you think.</p>"},{"slug":"how-i-built-a-simple-hardware-solution-for-my-daily-internet-woes","t":"How I Built a Simple Hardware Solution for My Daily Internet Woes","url":"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/how-i-built-a-simple-hardware-solution-for-my-daily-internet-woes/","author":"Milan Sony","d":"2026-07-07","tags":["Internet of Things"],"img":"https://pub-5911fd2c1cb64c8bb9ae3e5ffbb142b8.r2.dev/2026/07/msblogbgcoverimage.jpg","mins":3,"html":"<p>Hi folks 👋, this is my first blog. Well, I'm not that good at writing blogs, even though I enjoy reading them. I've thought about writing one many times, and here I am, finally writing my first blog.</p><p>Since my area of interest lies in tech and related stuff, this blog is about one such thing. It's actually a small product, or rather, a problem solver that I built recently.</p><p>Okay, let's get started.</p><p>At my home, the Wi-Fi network isn't that stable most of the time. Whenever the Wi-Fi connection gets interrupted, I have to either check the router's indicator light or run a ping command on my device to figure out whether it's just a high ping or an issue with the service provider. So, to solve this problem, I built a small project using an <code>ESP32</code>. <em>It's a microcontroller that can be programmed to control different electronic components and build IoT or automation projects.</em> I wired all the required components on a breadboard: an <code>RGB LED</code> for status indications, an <code>OLED display</code> to show the current status and information, and a <code>buzzer</code> for sound alerts. Then I wrote the code, fine-tuned it with the help of  ChatGPT, and voilà, the project was ready. 🎉</p><p>Here's how it works:</p><ul><li>When the ESP32 starts, it first tries to connect to my Wi-Fi network. If it isn't connected, it gives one long buzzer sound, the RGB LED turns red, and the ESP32 keeps trying to reconnect.</li><li>If the Wi-Fi is connected but there's no internet connection, the buzzer sounds three times, and the RGB LED starts blinking blue.</li><li>If the Wi-Fi is connected but the ping is high (I've set the condition to more than 200ms, calculated using the average of three ping requests), the RGB LED blinks yellow.</li><li>If the Wi-Fi is connected and stable, there is no LED indication or buzzer sound.</li></ul><p>All these checks run every 5 seconds. The current status is also displayed on the OLED screen. I also added a simple web dashboard so I can check the same information from any device connected to the Wi-Fi.</p><p>I'm not going to explain the code here because it's pretty straightforward, and this blog isn't about the code. I found a small problem at home, a repetitive task I had to do almost every day. So I thought of finding a solution, and I built this project. That's it. Like this, you can also build or do something of your own, even if it's a small thing. 🍀</p><p>I know it's a simple project. But <em>it's better to build something than do nothing, right? </em>The important thing is that this project serves a purpose for me now. I've been using it for the past few days, and it's genuinely helpful. Just by looking at the LED indication or hearing the buzzer, I can understand what's wrong with my Wi-Fi without having to check the router or run a ping command from my room. Also, if the Wi-Fi goes down, I don't have to keep checking my laptop or phone to see whether it's back online. If I'm working or doing something else, I can simply switch to mobile data and switch back to Wi-Fi once it's stable again. Of course, there are many other Wi-Fi monitoring projects that provide logs, graphs, analytics, and a lot more. But I wanted to keep this simple, no unnecessary features or fancy stuff. Maybe I'll add those later. 😉</p><p>Thank you for reading this short blog.</p><p>Have a nice day! ✨</p>"},{"slug":"building-reelity-with-ffmpeg-wasm","t":"Building Reelity: A Browser-First Approach to AI Reel Generation","url":"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/building-reelity-with-ffmpeg-wasm/","author":"Arjun Krishna","d":"2026-06-26","tags":["Project @ Inovus","Web Development"],"img":"https://pub-5911fd2c1cb64c8bb9ae3e5ffbb142b8.r2.dev/2026/06/hereby-attaching-the-screenshots-of-a-pr_s81omsHGVe2PeNGMQ-pNpw_ni1iUV6eSyCLN9vNcey87w.jpg","mins":7,"html":"<p>There are projects that begin with months of planning, carefully written roadmaps and feature lists. Then there are projects that begin with a message from a friend on a Friday evening.</p><p>Reelity belonged to the second category.</p><p>A friend sent me a reference video on a Friday evening and asked if we could <strong>build a platform that generated the kind of funny, intentionally low-effort promotional reels</strong> you often come across on Instagram and TikTok. The idea was simple: <u>give it a product, and it would create something that looked like it belonged in your social media feed</u>.</p><p>I spent the night exploring the idea, and by Saturday morning I had a working prototype. I had never built anything involving video generation before, but the challenge was interesting enough that I couldn't stop once I got started.</p><p>My first instinct was to think about the technology. Which AI model should I use? Which video generation API would fit best?</p><p>It felt like the obvious place to begin. But it wasn't.</p><h2 id=\"looking-at-the-problem-before-the-solution\">Looking at the Problem Before the Solution</h2><p>Instead of opening documentation for <a href=\"https://aistudio.google.com/models/veo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Veo</a> or <a href=\"https://openai.com/index/sora/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sora</a>, I spent time watching the reference video itself, again and again. Eventually, I stopped seeing it as a finished video and started seeing it as individual layers. </p><blockquote>There was a <u>background clip</u>, a <u>caption carrying the joke</u>, a <u>reaction GIF</u> placed at exactly the right moment and <u>music tying everything together</u>. None of those pieces were particularly sophisticated on their own. The humour came entirely from how they were combined.</blockquote><p>Before going any further, here's the style of reels that inspired the project.</p>\n\n\n\n\n  \n    \n  \n\n  \n    \n  \n\n  \n    \n  \n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"when-every-reel-became-json\">When Every Reel Became JSON</h2><p>The breakthrough came when I stopped thinking about the output as a video and started thinking about it as structured data. The final result might be an MP4, but what the renderer actually needed was a description of how to build it.</p><p>That idea became the foundation of the project. Instead of generating videos, the AI generated a JSON specification containing the joke, the caption, the media assets and instructions for putting everything together. The renderer didn't need to understand products or humour anymore—it simply had to follow the specification.</p><pre><code>{\n  \"format\": \"reaction\",\n  \"productName\": \"Echobit\",\n  \"siteUrl\": \"https://echobits.xyz\",\n\n  \"concept\": \"Someone realizes they zoned out the entire meeting but Echobit already transcribed and summarized everything in their language.\",\n  \"caption\": \"pov: you spaced out the whole meeting but Echobit got every word in Hindi 😭\",\n\n  \"accentColor\": \"#6C5CE7\",\n\n  \"background\": {\n    \"kind\": \"video\",\n    \"query\": \"office video call\",\n    \"url\": \"https://videos.pexels.com/video-files/8348784/8348784-sd_506_960_25fps.mp4\",\n    \"source\": \"pexels\"\n  },\n\n  \"reaction\": {\n    \"query\": \"phew that was close relief\",\n    \"url\": \"https://media1.giphy.com/media/v1.Y2lkPTNiYTc3MGY5dXdyZGp5bXNpODljbmhqY3IzdXZuZWh0MmZkY255M2EyZzlxOXhvcCZlcD12MV9naWZzX3NlYXJjaCZjdD1n/NWoJQ8peVE78A/giphy.gif\",\n    \"source\": \"giphy\"\n  },\n\n  \"audio\": {\n    \"vibe\": \"dramatic relief beat drop\",\n    \"title\": \"AleXZavesa - Deadly depth (Trance Dramatic)\",\n    \"url\": \"https://prod-1.storage.jamendo.com/?trackid=1336309&amp;format=mp32\",\n    \"source\": \"openverse\"\n  }\n}\n</code></pre><p>Once I had that abstraction, everything else naturally fell into place. <a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/api/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Pexels</a> supplied background videos, <a href=\"https://developers.giphy.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Giphy</a> handled reaction GIFs and <a href=\"https://api.openverse.org/v1/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Openverse</a> became the source for music. The AI's job was simply to generate the recipe. Everything else became deterministic.</p><h2 id=\"designing-for-failure\">Designing for Failure</h2><p>One lesson I've slowly learned from building side projects is that dependencies fail. APIs go down. Credits run out. Models change.</p><blockquote><a href=\"https://claude.ai/new\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Anthropic's Claude</a> was my first choice because I genuinely liked the quality of its responses, but I also knew I wouldn't have enough credits to comfortably build the entire application around it. That forced me to solve a problem I usually postpone until much later.</blockquote><p>Instead of tightly coupling Reelity to a single provider, I introduced the <a href=\"https://ai-sdk.dev/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Vercel AI SDK</a> as an abstraction layer. Anthropic became the preferred model, <a href=\"https://gemini.google.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Gemini</a> became another provider, <a href=\"https://www.cloudflare.com/products/workers-ai/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Cloudflare Workers AI</a> joined as an additional fallback and, eventually, I even wrote a simple heuristic generator that could produce something usable without relying on any language model at all.</p><p>Ironically, the abstraction worked so well that I didn't realise my Anthropic credits had quietly run out. The application had switched to its fallback without me noticing.</p><h2 id=\"rethinking-the-renderer\">Rethinking the Renderer</h2><p>When I started building Reelity, <a href=\"https://www.remotion.dev/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Remotion</a> was my first choice. I even put together a small prototype to understand how the rendering pipeline would work. It's an incredibly capable framework, and I could have easily deployed it on <a href=\"https://www.oracle.com/in/cloud/free/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Oracle Cloud</a>, <a href=\"https://render.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Render</a> or <a href=\"https://www.koyeb.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Koyeb</a> as a separate rendering service.</p><p>But somewhere along the way, I made a different decision. I wanted the entire application to live on <a href=\"https://cloudflare.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Cloudflare</a>.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.cloudflare.com/products/containers/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Cloudflare Containers</a> briefly crossed my mind as well, but if I was going to run a container anyway, I already had other platforms for that. The more interesting challenge was seeing whether I could build the entire application without needing one.</p><blockquote>Once I had settled on the JSON approach, I found myself questioning the rendering pipeline as well. <strong>Remotion was still a perfectly valid choice, but it also meant introducing a dedicated rendering service.</strong> At that point, I had already decided that <u>I wanted the entire project to live on Cloudflare, and that decision pushed me to look for a different approach</u>.</blockquote><p>At this point, the technical pieces had mostly fallen into place, and the project had started feeling less like an experiment and more like an application.</p><figure><img src=\"https://pub-5911fd2c1cb64c8bb9ae3e5ffbb142b8.r2.dev/2026/06/image.png\" alt=\"Building Reelity: A Browser-First Approach to AI Reel Generation\" loading=\"lazy\"><img src=\"https://pub-5911fd2c1cb64c8bb9ae3e5ffbb142b8.r2.dev/2026/06/image-1.png\" alt=\"Building Reelity: A Browser-First Approach to AI Reel Generation\" loading=\"lazy\"><img src=\"https://pub-5911fd2c1cb64c8bb9ae3e5ffbb142b8.r2.dev/2026/06/image-2.png\" alt=\"Building Reelity: A Browser-First Approach to AI Reel Generation\" loading=\"lazy\"><img src=\"https://pub-5911fd2c1cb64c8bb9ae3e5ffbb142b8.r2.dev/2026/06/image-3.png\" alt=\"Building Reelity: A Browser-First Approach to AI Reel Generation\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption><p>(1). Reelity landing page (2). Chat interface generating a promotional reel (3). Chat interface showing the generated reel and AI-selected composition details (mobile view) (4). Gallery of promotional reels created with Reelity</p></figcaption></figure><hr>⚠️<strong>A small heads-up</strong><br>The live demo is powered by AI services with limited credits, so I can't guarantee it will always be available. <strong>If reel generation isn't working when you try it, the API tokens have most likely been exhausted.</strong>\n            \n            \n                \n                \n                    \n                    \n                        \n                            <p>Explore the application in your browser and see how it generates promotional reels from a simple conversation.</p>\n                        \n                    \n                    \n                        <a href=\"https://reelity.arjunkrishna.dev/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">\n                            Open Reelity →\n                        </a>\n                        \n                    \n                \n            \n        <h2 id=\"letting-the-browser-do-the-heavy-lifting\">Letting the Browser Do the Heavy Lifting</h2><p>Around that time, I remembered reading a Hacker News discussion about FFmpeg compiled to WebAssembly. Reelity finally gave me a reason to try it.</p><p>Instead of introducing a dedicated rendering service, <strong>I used FFmpeg WASM to render the final video entirely inside the browser</strong>. The backend's responsibility was reduced to scraping the product website for context, generating the reel specification, coordinating AI providers and storing the completed reels.</p><p>That one decision ended up shaping the entire architecture. <u>Rendering no longer consumed backend resources, and as more people generated reels, the workload naturally scaled with their own devices instead of my infrastructure.</u> For a project that started as a weekend experiment, it felt like a surprisingly elegant way to solve the problem.</p><h2 id=\"building-on-cloudflare\">Building on Cloudflare</h2><p>With rendering moved into the browser, the backend became surprisingly small. <a href=\"https://hono.dev/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hono</a> handled the API, while <a href=\"https://vuejs.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Vue</a> was bundled into the same Worker and <a href=\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/static-assets/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">served as static assets</a>. <u>The application looked like two separate projects during development, but in production it behaved like a single deployment</u>.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.cloudflare.com/products/d1/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">D1</a> stores conversations and generated specifications, while <a href=\"https://www.cloudflare.com/products/r2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">R2</a> acts as persistent storage for completed reels. That means a generated video doesn't disappear after rendering—it can be revisited, shared or embedded later. </p><p>To protect the application, I added <a href=\"https://www.cloudflare.com/products/turnstile/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Turnstile</a> to prevent automated abuse, <a href=\"https://www.cloudflare.com/products/rate-limiting/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rate limiting</a> to control excessive usage and <a href=\"https://www.ibm.com/think/topics/prompt-injection\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">a few safeguards against prompt injection</a>. None of these features are particularly exciting on their own, but together they make the application much more resilient.</p><p>One of the things I enjoyed most was how everything stayed within the same platform. There were no additional services to manage, no separate rendering infrastructure and no servers running in the background. It all fit together surprisingly well.</p><p>After all the iterations, Reelity was finally producing the kind of short promotional reels that had inspired the project from the very beginning.</p>\n\n\n\n\n  \n    \n  \n\n  \n    \n  \n\n  \n    \n  \n\n\n\n\n            \n            \n                \n                \n                    \n                    \n                        \n                            <p><em>The reels above were all generated using Reelity.</em><br><em>These are just a small selection—the gallery has many more.</em></p>\n                        \n                    \n                    \n                        <a href=\"https://reelity.arjunkrishna.dev/gallery\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">\n                            Explore the Reel Gallery →\n                        </a>\n                        \n                    \n                \n            \n        <h2 id=\"one-last-hurdle\">One Last Hurdle</h2><p>There was one implementation detail that caught me completely by surprise.</p><p>The <a href=\"https://theproductguy.in/blogs/ffmpeg-browser-guide/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">FFmpeg WebAssembly core is roughly 30 MB</a>, which meant loading it directly from a Worker wasn't practical. My first attempt used the version published on <code>unpkg</code>, but I kept running into issues because of the Worker's asset limits.</p><p>The solution turned out to be surprisingly simple. <u>I downloaded the FFmpeg core, uploaded it to an R2 bucket and served it from my own custom domain</u>. From the browser's perspective, everything still lived under the same origin, and the Worker only needed to tell the client where to fetch it from. It was a small change, but it made the entire rendering pipeline work reliably.</p><p>It wasn't a rendering problem—it was simply about getting the browser to load the FFmpeg runtime reliably.</p>\n            \n            \n                \n                \n                    \n                    \n                        \n                            <p>If you'd like to explore the implementation or build on it yourself, the entire project is available on GitHub.</p>\n                        \n                    \n                    \n                        <a href=\"https://github.com/decoded-cipher/reelity\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">\n                            View Source on GitHub →\n                        </a>\n                        \n                    \n                \n            \n        <figure><a href=\"https://github.com/decoded-cipher/reelity\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GitHub - decoded-cipher/reelity: AI-organized UGC video generator in a chat box. Cloudflare Workers, Vue, Claude API, ffmpeg.wasm<small>AI-organized UGC video generator in a chat box. Cloudflare Workers, Vue, Claude API, ffmpeg.wasm - decoded-cipher/reelity</small></a></figure><h2 id=\"looking-back\">Looking Back</h2><p>When I started building Reelity, I expected the interesting part to be AI. It turned out to be the architecture instead.</p><p>Building the project gave me an excuse to finally experiment with WebAssembly in a real application. More importantly, it challenged an assumption I had held for a long time—that video rendering naturally belonged on a server. Watching FFmpeg run entirely inside the browser and seeing the architecture become simpler because of it was easily the most rewarding part of the project.</p><p>Reelity began as a weekend experiment inspired by a friend's message. Along the way, it became a reminder that sometimes the most interesting solutions come not from adding more technology, but from using the right technology in a different place.</p>"},{"slug":"nodrix-a-dream-that-refused-to-leave","t":"Nodrix: A Dream That Refused to Leave","url":"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/nodrix-a-dream-that-refused-to-leave/","author":"Arjun Krishna","d":"2026-06-10","tags":["Internet of Things","Project @ Inovus"],"img":"https://pub-5911fd2c1cb64c8bb9ae3e5ffbb142b8.r2.dev/2026/06/medium-cover-square.png","mins":7,"html":"<h2 id=\"the-beginning\">The Beginning</h2><img src=\"https://pub-5911fd2c1cb64c8bb9ae3e5ffbb142b8.r2.dev/2026/06/medium-cover-square.png\" alt=\"Nodrix: A Dream That Refused to Leave\" loading=\"lazy\"><p>When I joined <a href=\"https://kjcmt.ac.in/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kristu Jyoti College</a> for my BCA, I wasn’t particularly into electronics, and I wasn’t deeply into coding either. Like most students, I was still figuring out what interested me.</p><p>Things started changing when I became part of <a href=\"https://inovuslabs.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Inovus Labs</a>, the college IEDC supported by <a href=\"https://startupmission.kerala.gov.in/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kerala Startup Mission</a>. Inside the lab was a small IoT workspace filled with development boards, sensors, wires and half-finished projects. I had no idea how most of it worked, but I found myself constantly drawn towards it.</p><p>Around that time, I would often see <a href=\"https://jithinsha.in/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Jithin Sha</a> tinkering with different boards and hardware projects. Curiosity slowly took over. I started asking questions, and instead of simply answering them, he encouraged me to start experimenting on my own.</p><p>What followed was a lot of YouTube videos, documentation pages, trial and error, and countless hours spent figuring things out. Looking back, that small IoT lab ended up changing the course of my life far more than I ever expected.</p><h2 id=\"learning-by-breaking-things\">Learning by Breaking Things</h2><p>Over time, <a href=\"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/author/nikhil/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Nikhil</a> and <a href=\"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/author/indhuchoodan/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Indhuchoodan</a> joined the journey. Later came <a href=\"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/author/badhusha/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Badhusha</a>, <a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/milansony/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Milan</a>, <a href=\"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/author/amith/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Amith</a> and <a href=\"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/author/sane/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sane</a>. Together, we built countless projects — some successful, some abandoned halfway through, and some that failed in spectacular fashion. Most of our learning happened by trying things out, getting them wrong, and trying again.</p><p>Development boards were fried, components were sacrificed and a few capacitors met their end with a loud pop. There were even occasions when our experiments managed to trip sections of the college’s electrical system. Looking back, many of those moments are funnier than they were at the time.</p><blockquote>Through all of this, one person somehow remained remarkably patient: Kunjumon Chettan, the college electrician. While we were busy discovering new ways to break things, he was usually the one helping us put them back together.</blockquote><h2 id=\"the-problem-we-kept-facing\">The Problem We Kept Facing</h2><p>No matter what we were building, every IoT project eventually arrived at the same question:</p><blockquote>“Where do we send this data?”</blockquote><p>For a long time, the answer was <a href=\"https://www.blynk.io/blog/what-will-happen-to-the-legacy-blynk-platform\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Blynk</a>. It felt magical. Within minutes, a project could have a cloud backend, dashboards, graphs and controls. It allowed us to focus on building hardware rather than infrastructure.</p><p>When <a href=\"https://www.blynk.io/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Blynk 2.0</a> arrived, we were excited. But over time, the free tier became more restrictive and features gradually moved behind paid plans. There was nothing wrong with that — platforms need revenue to survive. The problem was that we were students building experimental projects with limited budgets.</p><p>So we adapted. Some projects moved to <a href=\"https://cloud.arduino.cc/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Arduino IoT Cloud</a>. Others used <a href=\"https://firebase.google.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Firebase</a>. A few relied on <a href=\"https://www.mongodb.com/lp/cloud/atlas/try4-reg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MongoDB</a>, custom dashboards and WebSockets. The tools changed, but the problem remained the same.</p><p>Every new project seemed to begin with rebuilding the same cloud layer all over again.</p><h2 id=\"a-question-that-stayed-behind\">A Question That Stayed Behind</h2><p>I don’t remember exactly when it happened, but I remember the conversation. We were working on a project when Nikhil casually asked a question.</p><blockquote>“Can we build something like Blynk ourselves, so that we’re not limited by these free tiers?”</blockquote><p>Nobody treated it seriously — not even the person who asked it. It wasn’t a proposal or a plan. Like thousands of other ideas discussed inside the lab, the conversation ended and everyone moved on.</p><p>To be honest, I didn’t think much about it either. But somehow, the idea stayed. It sat quietly in the back of my mind for years, waiting for the right tools, the right experience and perhaps the right time to resurface.</p><p>Years passed, projects came and went, college ended and life moved forward. Yet every now and then, that question would quietly return.</p><h2 id=\"cloudflare-changed-everything\">Cloudflare Changed Everything</h2><p>Over the years, as we continued building projects, we also found ourselves constantly jumping between cloud platforms. Like most students, we didn’t have money to spend on cloud infra, so free tiers often decided where a project would be deployed.</p><p>The journey started with Heroku. Then came Render, Vercel, Netlify, Firebase, Supabase, Digital Ocean and many others. Whenever a platform offered something useful within its free tier, we explored it. Even today, I find myself experimenting with platforms like <a href=\"https://www.oracle.com/in/cloud/free/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Oracle Cloud</a>, <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/free/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AWS</a> and <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/free\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Google Cloud</a>.</p><p>Somewhere along the way, <a href=\"https://cloudflare.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Cloudflare</a> became my default choice.</p><p>What fascinated me wasn’t just any single product. It was the ecosystem they were quietly building. Every year seemed to introduce another piece of infrastructure — Workers, KV, Durable Objects, D1, R2, Queues and realtime communication primitives. Individually, each service solved a specific problem. Together, they looked suspiciously like the building blocks of the platform we once wished existed.</p><p>One day, the realization finally hit me. Everything required to build something like Blynk was already there.</p><hr><h2 id=\"nodrix\">Nodrix</h2><p>That realization became Nodrix.</p><p>At its core, <a href=\"https://nodrix.live/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Nodrix</a> is the IoT platform we wanted when we were students building projects at Inovus Labs. It is built around a simple belief that developers should not have to choose between surrendering their data to a hosted IoT cloud and maintaining servers just to monitor a sensor.</p><figure><a href=\"https://nodrix.live/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"https://cdn-images-1.medium.com/max/1600/1*y8twsxBQaBJXAWfBhiN3uA.png\" alt=\"Nodrix: A Dream That Refused to Leave\" loading=\"lazy\"></a></figure>\n            \n            \n                \n                \n                    \n                    \n                        \n                            <p>Your own IoT cloud — open source, running entirely in your <strong>Cloudflare</strong> account.<br>From deploy to your first live sensor reading in minutes.</p>\n                        \n                    \n                    \n                        <a href=\"https://nodrix.live/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">\n                            Click here to open Nodrix\n                        </a>\n                        \n                    \n                \n            \n        <p>Nodrix is an <a href=\"https://github.com/decoded-cipher/nodrix\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">open-source</a> IoT platform built entirely on Cloudflare. Devices can send telemetry over HTTPS or WebSockets, dashboards update in realtime, automations can react to events, and integrations can connect with external services. More importantly, it removes much of the setup and ceremony that often makes IoT platforms feel intimidating.</p><figure><img src=\"https://pub-5911fd2c1cb64c8bb9ae3e5ffbb142b8.r2.dev/2026/06/launch-poster-2.png\" alt=\"Nodrix: A Dream That Refused to Leave\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><p>One of the design decisions I am happiest about is the removal of unnecessary complexity. Early versions followed the traditional IoT model of devices containing variables. Eventually I realised that users don’t actually care about creating devices before seeing their data. They care about getting their first sensor reading on a dashboard. So the platform was simplified. Data arrives, variables appear and the platform adapts around the user instead of the other way around.</p><h2 id=\"a-product-shaped-by-many-projects\">A Product Shaped by Many Projects</h2><p>Over the years, every project, workplace and experiment left behind lessons that eventually found their way into Nodrix.</p><p>The idea of embeddable widgets came from observing <a href=\"https://www.mongodb.com/products/platform/atlas-charts\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MongoDB Charts</a> and how easily visualizations could be integrated into external applications. Building those widgets as <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Web_components\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Web Components</a> comes from my time at <a href=\"https://airtory.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Airtory</a>, where I spent a lot of time working with custom web components.</p><figure><img src=\"https://cdn-images-1.medium.com/max/1600/1*4FOeuprPtfAUZvaWucpqpQ.png\" alt=\"Nodrix: A Dream That Refused to Leave\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><p>The visual automation builder has roots in another project of mine called Flareo (more on that later). Although that project was paused, many of the lessons learned while designing visual pipelines eventually found their way into Nodrix. The automation and integrations system was heavily influenced by <a href=\"https://n8n.io/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">n8n</a>, while the widget designs draw inspiration from platforms we have used for years, particularly <a href=\"https://cloud.arduino.cc/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Arduino IoT Cloud</a> and <a href=\"https://thingsboard.io/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ThingsBoard</a>.</p><p>Looking back, Nodrix feels like the natural intersection of everything I have learned while building software, hardware and developer tools over the years.</p><h2 id=\"why-single-tenant-matters\">Why Single-Tenant Matters</h2><p>Perhaps the most important thing about Nodrix is not a feature at all.</p><p>Most IoT platforms are shared services. You create an account, connect your devices and your data lives inside somebody else’s platform.</p><figure><img src=\"https://cdn-images-1.medium.com/max/1600/1*UHy2Nh5iIBPtZUlb-171Fw.png\" alt=\"Nodrix: A Dream That Refused to Leave\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><p>Nodrix takes a different approach.</p><p>With a single click, the entire platform deploys into your own Cloudflare account. The databases, storage, APIs and compute resources all live there. I don’t host your deployment, I don’t have access to your telemetry and there is no central Nodrix cloud where everyone’s data is collected.</p><p>That decision shaped almost every part of the project.</p><p>It means every deployment is independent. Nodrix is simply the software; the platform itself belongs to the people who deploy it.</p><h2 id=\"looking-ahead\">Looking Ahead</h2><p><a href=\"https://github.com/decoded-cipher/nodrix/releases/tag/v1.0.0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Version 1.0.0</a> is only the beginning.</p><figure><a href=\"https://github.com/decoded-cipher/nodrix\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GitHub - decoded-cipher/nodrix: The IoT platform Cloudflare didn’t build. Telemetry, realtime dashboards, and automations on Workers + D1 + R2 + Durable Objects — in your own Cloudflare account.<small>The IoT platform Cloudflare didn’t build. Telemetry, realtime dashboards, and automations on Workers + D1 + R2 + Durable Objects — in your own Cloudflare account. - decoded-cipher/nodrix</small></a></figure><p>There are already plans for native <strong>MQTT support</strong>, additional device protocols, fleet management, <strong>over-the-air</strong> (OTA) firmware updates, <strong>external database connectivity</strong> and <strong>deeper AI integrations</strong>. The platform <em>already includes a native MCP server</em>, but I believe AI can play a much bigger role in helping users understand data, create automations and manage infrastructure.</p><p>Like the project itself, the roadmap is heavily shaped by conversations with builders, makers and developers who use it.</p><h2 id=\"launch-day\">Launch Day</h2><p>On June 9, 2026, Nodrix officially launched on <a href=\"https://producthunt.com/products/nodrix\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Product Hunt</a>.</p><p>For me, the launch was less about releasing a product and more about seeing an old idea finally come to life.</p>\n\n<a href=\"https://www.producthunt.com/products/nodrix?embed=true&amp;utm_source=badge-featured&amp;utm_medium=badge&amp;utm_campaign=badge-nodrix\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"https://api.producthunt.com/widgets/embed-image/v1/featured.svg?post_id=1157071&amp;theme=light&amp;t=1781096224141\" alt=\"Nodrix: A Dream That Refused to Leave\" loading=\"lazy\"></a>\n\n\n\n<img src=\"https://ph-files.imgix.net/d4a69a26-b975-4fa7-b4a7-b48070f2aaf2.png?auto=compress,format&amp;codec=mozjpeg&amp;cs=strip&amp;fit=crop&amp;h=80&amp;w=80\" alt=\"Nodrix: A Dream That Refused to Leave\" loading=\"lazy\"><h3>Nodrix</h3><p>Your own IoT cloud, deployed to your Cloudflare account</p><a href=\"https://www.producthunt.com/products/nodrix?embed=true&amp;utm_source=embed&amp;utm_medium=post_embed\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Check it out on Product Hunt →</a>\n\n<p>What started as a casual question during a project discussion had turned into something real. Not because there was a grand plan from the beginning, but because the right tools, experiences and opportunities gradually came together over time.</p><h2 id=\"looking-back\">Looking Back</h2><p>When I look at Nodrix today, I don’t just see an IoT platform.</p><p>I see a small IoT lab inside Inovus Labs. I see friends building projects long after classes ended. I see sensors, jumper wires, burnt components and endless curiosity. I see mentors who encouraged us to experiment and people who patiently tolerated the mistakes that came with learning.</p><p>In many ways, Nodrix is simply the next chapter of that journey.</p><p>Sometimes the most interesting projects aren’t born from business plans or market research. Sometimes they begin as a passing thought, get forgotten for years and quietly wait for the right tools, the right experience and the right moment to come back.</p><p>Nodrix just happened to be one of those ideas.</p>"},{"slug":"college-live-signage-platform","t":"How We Turned an Old Signage TV Into a Live Content Platform","url":"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/college-live-signage-platform/","author":"Arjun Krishna","d":"2026-05-23","tags":["Project @ Inovus"],"img":"https://pub-5911fd2c1cb64c8bb9ae3e5ffbb142b8.r2.dev/2026/05/i-want-a-feature-image-for-a-blog-that-i_zcAIfCJxV1CgbI179BhNhg_rSykqnQ5T46AcSNk2qM7Cw_sd.jpeg","mins":7,"html":"<p>Near the entrance corridor of our college stood a portrait-oriented TV powered by <a href=\"https://www.lg.com/in/business/information-display/digital-signage/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LG WebOS Smart Signage</a>. It was originally meant to display announcements, event details, notices, and promotional content for the college. Like most digital signage systems, it looked exciting during the initial days. People uploaded content regularly, events were showcased, and announcements were updated.</p><p>But slowly, the updates stopped.</p><p>The hardware itself was never the problem. The real issue was that the workflow depended entirely on manually updating the content. Eventually, nobody wanted to keep logging into the signage dashboard just to replace an image or upload another video. Over time, the same old promotional content kept playing repeatedly for months.</p><p>At one point, we even noticed that the display was not being turned on consistently anymore because everybody already knew what was going to appear on it.</p><p>Around the same time, we at Inovus Labs were thinking about increasing the reach of <a href=\"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><strong>Inovus Blogs</strong></a> and the <a href=\"https://open.spotify.com/show/4QXEeK8ZWxewcXuLKCRR8R?si=67fdbaeef2b74ed6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><strong>Inora Podcast</strong></a>. That was when we started looking at the existing signage system differently..</p><figure></figure><p>We did not know exactly how we were going to use it at that point. But we knew the display had visibility and foot traffic. The challenge was figuring out how to transform a static signage system into something that could showcase our content without requiring manual maintenance every day.</p><h2 id=\"from-static-posters-to-browser-experiments\">From Static Posters to Browser Experiments</h2><p>While discussing the signage platform with the college lab admin who used to manage the display, we discovered that the LG WebOS signage system could open external web URLs directly.</p><p>That single detail changed the direction of the project.</p><p>The first experiment was simple — a static webpage hosted on <a href=\"https://pages.github.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GitHub Pages</a> that displayed a fullscreen poster instead of manually uploaded media on signage dashboard.</p><p>The second version evolved into a 3D carousel-style interface built using HTML, CSS transforms, and JavaScript animations that continuously rotated image cards on the screen.</p>\n\n\n  \n\n\n<p>The third attempt was far more ambitious. We built a Vue (Vite)-based webpage with animated bubbles, moving particles, floating nodes, and canvas-rendered network-like visuals to make the display feel more dynamic and alive.</p><p>It worked properly on modern systems, but the TV could not handle it reliably.</p><blockquote>The Chromium engine inside the LG WebOS signage platform was extremely old, and the Vite-based CSR application frequently failed to render properly, often ending up on a white screen with application errors due to unsupported browser features.</blockquote><p>After that, the entire idea went into a halt for almost a year. Like many side experiments at Inovus Labs, we eventually moved on and got busy with other projects, while the kiosk idea quietly stayed in the background.</p><h2 id=\"rebuilding-the-system-around-automation\">Rebuilding the System Around Automation</h2><p>Very recently, we revisited the project with a completely different approach. Instead of focusing on visually heavy interfaces, the goal became building a stable automated content pipeline that could reliably run on the signage hardware for long periods without manual intervention.</p><p>The current version of the project is available here:</p><figure><a href=\"https://github.com/inovus-labs/kiosk.inovuslabs.org\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GitHub - inovus-labs/kiosk.inovuslabs.org: A fully automated digital signage system for Inovus Labs — transforms live content (blogs &amp; podcasts) into a continuously updating kiosk display with zero manual intervention.<small>A fully automated digital signage system for Inovus Labs — transforms live content (blogs &amp;amp; podcasts) into a continuously updating kiosk display with zero manual intervention. - inovus-labs/kio…</small></a></figure><p>The kiosk now runs a very lightweight fullscreen web application optimized specifically for a portrait-oriented <code>1080×1920</code> display. Instead of manually uploading posters or videos, the system pulls the content from live sources like Inovus Blogs and the Inora podcast RSS feed — Spotify for Podcasters.</p>\n\n\nflowchart TD\n    %% ── Sources ─────────────────────────────────\n    A[(Ghost CMS)]:::source\n    E[(Podcast RSS feed)]:::source\n\n    %% ── Build &amp; deploy pipeline ─────────────────\n    W[Cloudflare Worker]:::cf\n    B[GitHub Actions]:::gh\n    C[GitHub Pages]:::gh\n    D[Portrait TV<br>1080 × 1920]:::tv\n\n    %% ── Post-deploy side channel ────────────────\n    BR[Cloudflare Browser<br>Rendering API]:::cf\n    F[Discord]:::discord\n\n    A --&gt;|Webhook| W\n    W --&gt;|repository_dispatch| B\n    E --&gt;|Spotify for Podcasters| B\n    B --&gt;|Deploys to gh-pages| C\n    C --&gt;|Auto-refresh every 30 min| D\n\n    B -.-&gt;|Capture slide| BR\n    BR -.-&gt;|PNG| B\n    B -.-&gt;|Slide PNG| F\n\n    classDef source  fill:#FEF3C7,stroke:#F59E0B,color:#78350F;\n    classDef cf      fill:#FFE4CC,stroke:#F38020,color:#7C2D12;\n    classDef gh      fill:#E5E7EB,stroke:#374151,color:#111827;\n    classDef tv      fill:#6C63FF,stroke:#4F46E5,color:#FFFFFF;\n    classDef discord fill:#5865F2,stroke:#4752C4,color:#FFFFFF;\n\n\n<p>The publishing pipeline itself became event-driven. Whenever a blog is published, updated, or unpublished in Ghost CMS, a webhook triggers a Cloudflare Worker. The worker validates the request and sends a <code>repository_dispatch</code> event to GitHub Actions, which rebuilds the kiosk, fetches the latest content from Ghost and podcast feeds, regenerates the slides, and deploys the updated build automatically to the <code>gh-pages</code> branch.</p><h2 id=\"designing-around-the-hardware\">Designing Around the Hardware</h2><p>Since the LG WebOS signage platform runs on an older Chromium engine internally, the frontend had to avoid heavy client-side rendering and browser-dependent features.</p><p>The kiosk runs as a lightweight fullscreen web application optimized for a <code>1080×1920</code> portrait display. <u>Content rotates every 10 seconds</u> using simple fade transitions and the <u>page itself reloads once in 30 mins</u>. Blog slides contain featured images, excerpts, branding elements, and QR codes, while podcast slides include episode artwork, duration, release dates, and Spotify links.</p><figure><img src=\"https://pub-5911fd2c1cb64c8bb9ae3e5ffbb142b8.r2.dev/2026/05/1000233920.jpg\" alt=\"How We Turned an Old Signage TV Into a Live Content Platform\" loading=\"lazy\"><img src=\"https://pub-5911fd2c1cb64c8bb9ae3e5ffbb142b8.r2.dev/2026/05/1000233919.jpg\" alt=\"How We Turned an Old Signage TV Into a Live Content Platform\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption><p>The digital signage at our college lobby - running automated content</p></figcaption></figure><p>In the initial design of this approach, the kiosk directly consumed the Ghost Content API and relied on a scheduled daily build. The problem was the delay — <u>if a blog was published during the day, either we had to wait until the next scheduled build or manually trigger a deployment for the content to appear on the kiosk</u>.</p><p>This was later solved by introducing a Cloudflare Worker. Ghost webhooks now trigger the worker on <code>post.published</code>, <code>post.updated</code>, and <code>post.unpublished</code> events, which then dispatch a GitHub Actions workflow to rebuild and redeploy the kiosk automatically.</p><p>Another important detail is that all external content is fetched only during build time. Once deployed, the kiosk runs entirely as a static site without continuously pulling data from Ghost, Spotify feeds, or other dependent APIs on every refresh.</p><p>The deployment pipeline also generates <u>automated screenshots</u> after every build. <a href=\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/browser-run/quick-actions/screenshot-endpoint/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Cloudflare Browser Rendering</a> opens: <code>https://kiosk.inovuslabs.org/?screenshot=0</code> and captures a <code>1080×1920</code> screenshot of the latest slide and sends it to our <a href=\"https://join.inovuslabs.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Discord Server</a>, from where it is reshared to Instagram Stories and WhatsApp Statuses.</p><figure><img src=\"https://pub-5911fd2c1cb64c8bb9ae3e5ffbb142b8.r2.dev/2026/05/discord_post.png\" alt=\"How We Turned an Old Signage TV Into a Live Content Platform\" loading=\"lazy\"><img src=\"https://pub-5911fd2c1cb64c8bb9ae3e5ffbb142b8.r2.dev/2026/05/blogslide_01.png\" alt=\"How We Turned an Old Signage TV Into a Live Content Platform\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption><p>Social Share assets for a published blog — available on our Discord Server</p></figcaption></figure><p>Initially, this failed because the rendering API waits for network activity to become idle before capturing the page, while the kiosk continuously played background audio. To solve this, the kiosk switches into a <u>dedicated screenshot mode</u> using the <code>?screenshot=1</code> query parameter, <u>where audio, timers, and animations are disabled</u> for cleaner captures.</p><h2 id=\"qr-layer-and-analytics\">QR Layer and Analytics</h2><p>Every displayed blog or podcast contains dynamically generated QR codes that students can scan directly from the corridor display.</p><p>The QR URLs include <a href=\"https://ortto.com/learn/what-are-utm-parameters/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">UTM parameters</a> and attribution metadata, allowing traffic originating from the kiosk to be identified separately inside <a href=\"https://marketingplatform.google.com/about/analytics/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Google Analytics</a>. This made it possible to <u>measure how users interacted with content displayed</u> physically on the screen and <u>how many people continued reading articles</u> or <u>listening to podcasts on their phones after scanning the QR codes</u>.</p><h2 id=\"what-comes-next\">What Comes Next</h2><p>After deploying the kiosk across the campus, we started receiving surprisingly positive feedback. Students actually stopped, scanned QR codes, explored blogs, and noticed the content playing on the display.</p><figure>\n            \n                \n                \n                \n            \n            <figcaption><p>The digital signage - running automated content</p></figcaption>\n        </figure><p>But that also exposed a new problem.</p><p>Right now, the kiosk only knows how to publish blogs and podcasts. <u>If somebody wants to display announcements</u>, notices, event posters, schedules, or other content, <u>they still have to switch back to the original LG WebOS signage system</u> — which means <u>our automated content disappears entirely</u>.</p><blockquote>Since we already have a Cloudflare Worker and an event-driven pipeline in place, we are now exploring the idea of turning this into a lightweight CMS-based publishing platform where authorized members can publish different types of campus content alongside blogs and podcasts.</blockquote><p>Thanks to <a href=\"https://blog.inovuslabs.org/author/amith/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Amith</a> for the suggestion.</p><h2 id=\"more-than-a-kiosk-project\">More Than a Kiosk Project</h2><p>For almost a year, this project remained abandoned while we got busy with other things at Inovus Labs. What eventually made it work was not a new framework or a complicated architecture, but approaching the same problem again from a different angle.</p><p>In the end, the solution was surprisingly simple — Ghost webhooks, a Cloudflare Worker, GitHub Actions, a static build pipeline, and a lightweight frontend designed around the hardware constraints already in front of us.</p><p>More than the kiosk itself, the project became a reminder that innovation is not always about replacing systems or adding more complexity. Sometimes, it simply comes from reconnecting the right pieces differently.</p><blockquote>“It’s not always about building something new.<br>Sometimes, it’s about seeing existing systems differently.”</blockquote>"}];
